{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-8f71b1b6441bce1a", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1\tTransforming Keystroke Noise to Text:\n2\tSelf-Supervised Acoustic Eavesdropping Attacks on Keyboards\n3\tAtsunori Okada\n4\t\n5\tAkira Ito\n6\t\n7\tRei Ueno\n8\t\n9\tTohoku University\n10\tokada.atsunori@dc.tohoku.ac.jp\n11\t\n12\tTohoku University\n13\takira.ito.b1@tohoku.ac.jp\n14\t\n15\tKyoto University\n16\tueno.rei.2e@kyoto-u.ac.jp\n17\t\n18\tYuichi Hayashi\n19\t\n20\tNaofumi Homma\n21\t\n22\tNara Institute of Science and Technology\n23\tyu-ichi@is.naist.jp\n24\t\n25\tTohoku University\n26\tnaofumi.homma.c8@tohoku.ac.jp\n27\t\n28\tarXiv:2607.22094v1 [cs.CR] 24 Jul 2026\n29\t\n30\tAbstract\n31\tWe present a self-supervised acoustic eavesdropping attack that reconstructs typed text solely from keystroke sounds, without\n32\trequiring labeled data for the target device. The proposed attack enables stealthy eavesdropping in two real-world scenarios—physical spaces (public and semi-public) and online meetings. Our method combines unsupervised acoustic clustering\n33\twith Transformer-based language model inference and iterative self-training, enabling stable character inference under highly\n34\tuncertain acoustic-to-character mappings. We demonstrate that the proposed method achieves over 99% reconstruction accuracy with only 100–150 observed keystrokes under a close-proximity recording setup using a smartphone placed near the\n35\ttarget device, significantly outperforming prior unsupervised baselines in low-data regimes. We further evaluate robustness\n36\tacross multiple laptop platforms and in realistic acquisition channels, including distance recording from approximately 3 meters away on the same desk, through-the-wall eavesdropping with a contact microphone, and background keyboard noise in\n37\tonline conferencing systems. Across these scenarios, the proposed method achieves high reconstruction accuracy (often exceeding 90%) with approximately 150–250 observed keystrokes. These results indicate that accurate text reconstruction from\n38\tkeystroke sounds is feasible in practice under an audio-only setting, even with limited observed keystrokes and without requiring device-specific labeled data, highlighting a realistic and previously underestimated privacy risk.\n39\t\n40\t1. Introduction\n41\t\n42\tHowever, supervised acoustic methods often depend on\n43\tlabeled keystroke recordings collected in advance for the\n44\ttarget device or a closely matched environment [2, 14, 3],\n45\twhich is difficult to obtain in realistic attack scenarios\n46\tand can be sensitive to changes in the recording channel or keyboard (violating requirement (ii)). Unsupervised acoustic approaches remove the need for such profiling data, but they either rely on dedicated non-portable\n47\trecording setups, such as microphone arrays placed close\n48\tto the keyboard [34], or require substantially more observed keystrokes to achieve stable inference [43], making them impractical under limited observations (violating\n49\trequirement (iii)). Electromagnetic (EM) attacks leverage signal leakage from wired keyboards, where digital\n50\tinformation corresponding to keystrokes can be captured\n51\tfrom emanations along cables [36]. However, such attacks typically require specialized sensing equipment and\n52\tcareful measurement setups, limiting their practicality in\n53\treal-world scenarios (violating requirement (i)). Wireless\n54\tsensing approaches, such as those based on WiFi channel state information (CSI) or beamforming feedback information (BFI), can infer typing behavior by analyzing\n55\tsubtle changes in wireless signal strength caused by finger\n56\tmovements [1, 18, 38, 15]. However, these approaches\n57\toften rely on favorable transmitter/receiver placement or\n58\taccess to wireless infrastructure, which may not be available to an attacker in realistic settings (violating requirement (i)). In addition, vision-based methods use cameras\n59\tto capture typing motions and recover keystrokes from visual observations [40, 31, 5]. However, they typically require a clear line of sight to the victim, which significantly\n60\trestricts their applicability in everyday environments (violating requirement (i)). As a result, existing studies do not\n61\tsimultaneously satisfy the aforementioned three requirements, which has been a major obstacle to practical key-\n62\t\n63\t1.1 Background\n64\tRecovering typed text from keyboard input poses a serious\n65\tthreat to user privacy and security, as it can expose sensitive information such as private communications, business discussions, and confidential documents. In modern\n66\tworking environments, people increasingly type on laptops in public and semi-public spaces, including cafes, libraries, public transportation (e.g., bullet trains and airplanes), and waiting lounges, where their keystroke signals may unintentionally leak through physical side channels (e.g., keystroke sounds and electromagnetic radiation). Furthermore, online meetings have rapidly become\n67\tcommon, and participants may type during calls while\n68\ttheir keystroke sounds are captured by their microphones.\n69\tThese daily scenarios make it possible for an adversary to\n70\tobserve side-channel signals generated by typing without\n71\trequiring direct access to the device or privileged system\n72\tinformation. Given this risk, an important question is what\n73\tconstitutes a practical keystroke eavesdropping attack in\n74\treal-world conditions. In this paper, we consider such an\n75\tattack to require three key properties: (i) minimal deployment constraints, (ii) no labeled data for the target device,\n76\tand (iii) high reconstruction accuracy from only a limited\n77\tnumber of observed keystrokes.\n78\tWhile existing studies have demonstrated that keystroke\n79\tinformation can be inferred from various side channels using different sensing modalities, these approaches do not\n80\tfully satisfy the aforementioned requirements for practical attacks. Acoustic attacks exploit the fact that each key\n81\tproduces a distinct sound pattern, enabling classification\n82\tof keystrokes from audio recordings [2, 43, 14, 3, 4, 9].\n83\t\n84\t1\n85\t\n86\t\fboard eavesdropping attacks.\n87\t\n88\tMinimal\n89\tdeployment\n90\t(a) Public spaces\n91\t\n92\t1.2 Our Contribution\n93\tTo address these limitations of existing approaches, we\n94\tpropose a self-supervised acoustic eavesdropping attack\n95\tdesigned to satisfy all three requirements. Figure 1 provides an overview of the considered attack scenarios and\n96\thighlights the key properties of our approach. Our main\n97\tidea is to leverage a Transformer-based language model\n98\tto stabilize sequence prediction. Specifically, we employ\n99\ta character-level BERT model to infer the most plausible\n100\tcharacter sequence from a noisy cluster sequence, using\n101\tambiguity-aware embeddings that encode the probabilistic correspondences between clusters and characters. Because this method relies only on passively observed audio\n102\tand does not require specialized sensing hardware, it can\n103\toperate under minimal setup constraints (requirement (i)).\n104\tMoreover, by utilizing linguistic priors instead of supervised acoustic-to-character mappings, the method eliminates the need for labeled data for the target device (requirement (ii)). Finally, by leveraging contextual inference\n105\twith a Transformer-based language model, namely BERT\n106\tand LLM-based correction, the proposed method achieves\n107\taccurate reconstruction from a limited number of observed\n108\tkeystrokes, demonstrating strong performance in low-data\n109\tregimes (requirement (iii)).\n110\t\n111\tNo labeled\n112\tdata\n113\t\n114\t(c) Online meeting\n115\t\n116\tTyped text prediction\n117\t\n118\tAudio leakage\n119\t\n120\t(b) Through-wall\n121\t\n122\tHigh accuracy\n123\twith limited data\n124\t\n125\t…\n126\t\n127\tdear michael,\n128\tthis is a quick\n129\tfollow up regarding\n130\tour recent discussion.\n131\tafter reviewing the\n132\tnotes with you…\n133\t\n134\tSelf-supervised\n135\tinference\n136\tmodel\n137\t\n138\tAttacker\n139\t\n140\tFigure 1: Example attack scenarios for acoustic keyboard\n141\teavesdropping.\n142\t\n143\t2. Threat Model\n144\t2.1 Adversarial Goals\n145\tThe attacker’s goal is to reconstruct text typed by a victim\n146\ton a keyboard using only recorded keystroke sounds. We\n147\tfocus primarily on English natural-language sentences,\n148\twhile noting that some security-critical data, including\n149\tpasswords and credit card numbers, are strings that do not\n150\tfollow natural-language patterns. Accordingly, we primarily demonstrate eavesdropping attacks on natural-language\n151\ttext and then evaluate random-string estimation as a proxy\n152\tfor password recovery.\n153\t\n154\tTo validate the proposed attack, we evaluate the method\n155\tthrough close-proximity recording, cross-device tests, and\n156\trealistic recording scenarios. In a simplified recording\n157\tsetup, the proposed attack achieves more than 99% reconstruction accuracy using only about 100–150 keystrokes.\n158\tWe further evaluate the method under practical acquisition conditions with minimal setup constraints, including distant recording on the same desk, through-the-wall\n159\teavesdropping with a contact microphone, and audio captured through online conferencing systems. Experimental\n160\tresults demonstrate that the proposed attack satisfies all\n161\tthree requirements and that accurate text reconstruction\n162\tfrom keystroke acoustics is practical in real-world environments.\n163\t\n164\t2.2 Victim Conditions\n165\tWe assume a typical computer user typing English text\n166\tduring a typing session. We characterize the victim as follows:\n167\t• Character set. The victim types text composed of\n168\tthe following 29 symbols: lowercase letters a--z\n169\t(26 letters), space, comma (,), and period (.). For simplicity, the same character set assumption is used in\n170\tthe random-password experiment in Section 6.2.\n171\t\n172\tThe main contributions of this paper are summarized as\n173\tfollows:\n174\t\n175\t• Input device. The victim uses a standard physical\n176\tkeyboard integrated into a laptop. In our evaluation,\n177\twe consider multiple laptop platforms to assess the\n178\trobustness of the proposed method across different\n179\tkeyboard conditions. For more details, see Section 4\n180\tand 5.\n181\t\n182\t• We design real-world keyboard eavesdropping attack\n183\tscenarios and identify the requirements for practical\n184\tattacks.\n185\t\n186\t• Typing speed. We assume a typing speed of 240\n187\tcharacters per minute (CPM). Empirical studies report that typical users type at approximately 30–60\n188\twords per minute [8, 30], corresponding to about\n189\t180–360 CPM assuming six characters per word.\n190\tThis implies an average keystroke interval of roughly\n191\t200–400 ms. At these speeds, consecutive keystroke\n192\tsounds generally do not overlap, which is important\n193\tbecause the proposed attack relies on audio waveform\n194\tsegmentation. In other words, the proposed attack is\n195\tapplicable as long as consecutive keystroke sounds\n196\tdo not overlap.\n197\t\n198\t• We propose a novel self-supervised inference\n199\tpipeline that achieves significantly higher accuracy\n200\tthan prior unsupervised acoustic baselines across various settings.\n201\t• We demonstrate the robustness and practicality of\n202\tthe proposed approach across multiple devices and\n203\trealistic recording scenarios, including desk-surface\n204\trecording, through-the-wall acquisition, and online\n205\tconferencing audio.\n206\t\n207\t2\n208\t\n209\t\f2.3 Adversarial Assumptions and Constraints\n210\t\n211\tclustering process converts keystroke sounds into a discrete cluster-index sequence, in which each cluster is expected to correspond to one or more possible characters\n212\t(although the correspondence is initially unknown). Given\n213\tan audio waveform of a typing session, we first detect\n214\tkeystroke timings and segment the waveform into individual keystroke snippets (Section 3.2). From each snippet, we extract a high-dimensional acoustic feature vector\n215\t(Section 3.3). We then apply UMAP-based dimensionality reduction to improve clustering reliability under limited observations (Sections 3.4 and 3.5). We explicitly\n216\tidentify the space key using a distance threshold in the\n217\tdimensionality-reduced space because its acoustic characteristics are often distinctive (Section 3.6); these space positions stabilize the subsequent language-model decoding.\n218\tUsing a feedback learning loop, we then decode the cluster sequence into text by combining a cluster-to-character\n219\tmapping with language-model priors. We define and initialize a probabilistic mapping matrix that represents the\n220\tcorrespondence between clusters and characters and construct ambiguity-aware token embeddings from this matrix. A character-level BERT model then performs masked\n221\tlanguage modeling over the embedding sequence to infer\n222\tthe most likely character at each position (Section 3.7).\n223\tWe further refine the decoded text using an LLM-based\n224\tcontextual correction module (Section 3.8). To improve\n225\trobustness in low-data regimes, we incorporate iterative\n226\tself-training. We extract high-confidence spans where the\n227\tBERT output agrees with the LLM-corrected text, propagate pseudo-labels to the remaining keystrokes using label spreading in the acoustic feature space, and update\n228\tthe emission matrix in the feedback loop (Sections 3.9\n229\tand 3.10).\n230\t\n231\tThe attacker is assumed to observe the audio waveform\n232\tproduced by the victim’s typing but to have no other information. The audio waveform is captured using an off-theshelf microphone. To model a practical attack, we assume\n233\tthat the attacker operates under the following constraints:\n234\t• Limited environmental control and portable\n235\trecording setup. Depending on the situation, the attacker may be able to place a microphone, but cannot arbitrarily change the victim’s keyboard or device configuration. Although the microphone may be\n236\tplaced in a favorable position for low-noise recording, the attacker does not know its exact distance or\n237\tangle from the target device.\n238\t• No prior knowledge, no profiling, and no compromise of the target device. The attacker has no\n239\tdetailed knowledge about what keyboard the victim\n240\tuses and what type of English text the victim types.\n241\tThe attacker obtains neither the ground-truth typed\n242\ttext nor any labeled dataset that maps keys or characters to their corresponding acoustic signals. The\n243\tattacker does not use malware such as keyloggers,\n244\taccess OS-internal information, or abuse application\n245\tprivileges on the victim device.\n246\tThese constraints capture practical keyboard eavesdropping scenarios: opportunistic audio-only observation\n247\twithout any supervised data prepared in advance.\n248\t2.4 Practical Settings and Proposed Scenarios\n249\tIn this paper, we consider the following three microphone\n250\tsetups: (a) a portable voice recorder, such as a smartphone, (b) a portable contact microphone, and (c) laptop built-in microphone. We set the audio sampling rate\n251\tto 44.1 kHz, as is common in audio recording.\n252\t\n253\t3.2 Keystroke Segmentation\n254\tWe segment a continuous audio recording into individual keystroke waveforms; that is, we detect the timing of\n255\teach keystroke and extract a fixed-length window around\n256\teach detected event. Figure 3 displays an example of a\n257\tkeystroke audio waveform and its segmentation.\n258\t\n259\tWe consider three attack scenarios based on these microphone setups. Setups (a) and (b) enable distant\n260\trecording in (semi-)public spaces and through-the-wall\n261\trecording from an office or meeting room by placing the\n262\tvoice recorder or contact microphone on a desk or wall, respectively. Setup (c) targets online meetings. In this scenario, the attacker obtains keystroke sounds as background\n263\tnoise when the victim types during a meeting. Although\n264\tsome online conferencing systems support noise cancellation, this feature may be disabled, unavailable, or provided\n265\tonly as a paid feature. From an attacker’s perspective, such\n266\tconditions are sufficient to exploit keystroke sounds leaked\n267\tthrough meeting audio. This study demonstrates the feasibility of acoustic keyboard eavesdropping in these practical scenarios, raising a serious privacy concern.\n268\t\n269\tAutomatic keystroke detection. Given an audio waveform x(t), we compute its short-time Fourier transform\n270\t(STFT) with a window length of 1,024 samples and track\n271\tthe temporal change of the signal energy (i.e., sum of\n272\tthe squared power spectral density). Figure 3 also shows\n273\tthe short-window energy curve, which has a peak at\n274\tthe position of a keystroke. Based on this observation,\n275\twe use a peak-detection algorithm that identifies peaks\n276\tbased on prominence [6] to find candidate keystroke timings. Let P = {p1 , p2 , . . . , pN } be the detected peak\n277\tindices, where N is the number of detected keystrokes.\n278\tFor each pi , we extract a segment comprising consecutive L = 8,192 samples centered\u0001 at pi , i.e., si =\n279\txpi −L/2 , xpi −L/2+1 , . . . , xpi +L/2−1 . Keystroke sounds\n280\toften contain both press and release components; we focus on the press components and align segments around\n281\tthe press peak.\n282\t\n283\t3. Attack Design\n284\t3.1 Overview\n285\tFigure 2 presents the end-to-end pipeline of the proposed\n286\tattack for reconstructing text from keystroke sounds. The\n287\t3\n288\t\n289\t\fClustering process\n290\tAudio\n291\twaveform\n292\t\n293\tKeystroke\n294\tsegmentation\n295\tSection 3.2\n296\t\n297\tFeature extraction\n298\t（902-dim）\n299\tSection 3.3\n300\t\n301\tDimension reduction\n302\t（5-dim）\n303\t\n304\tClustering\n305\t\n306\tSection 3.4\n307\t\n308\tSection 3.5\n309\t\n310\tBERT\n311\tprediction\n312\tSection 3.7\n313\t\n314\tLLM-based\n315\tcorrection\n316\tSection 3.8\n317\t\n318\tClustering\n319\tseries\n320\t\n321\tAcoustic features\n322\t\n323\tFeedback learning\n324\tClustering\n325\tseries\n326\t\n327\tSpace key\n328\tdetection\n329\tSection 3.6\n330\t\n331\tSelf-supervised\n332\ttraining\n333\tSection 3.9\n334\t\n335\tUpdate\n336\tM-matrix\n337\tSection 3.10\n338\t\n339\tLLM-based\n340\tcorrection\n341\t\n342\tPredicted text\n343\t\n344\tFeedback loop\n345\t\n346\tFigure 2: Proposed attack pipeline.\n347\tby concatenating all features as\n348\t\u0002\n349\tmax\n350\tfi = mmean\n351\t, mvar\n352\t, mmin\n353\t, mmed\n354\t,\n355\ti\n356\ti , mi\n357\ti\n358\ti\n359\t\u0003\n360\t⊤\n361\tzi , ∆i , ∆2i\n362\t∈ R902 ,\n363\t\n364\tPush peak\n365\t\n366\tSegment\n367\t\n368\tRelease peak\n369\t\n370\tDetection point\n371\t\n372\t(1)\n373\t\n374\twhich is the input to the subsequent dimensionality reduction stage. Note that we standardize the feature vectors\n375\tacross keystroke sound samples before the dimensionality\n376\treduction.\n377\t\n378\tFigure 3: Example of audio segmentation using the waveform (top) and short-window energy curve (bottom).\n379\t\n380\t3.4 Dimensionality Reduction\n381\tIn this work, we target eavesdropping attacks that operate\n382\twith only a limited number of observations (e.g., 100–200\n383\tkeystrokes), where reliable inference must be achieved\n384\tunder severe data constraints. In this regime, the feature dimensionality (902) is substantially larger than the\n385\tnumber of observations. This imbalance may make the\n386\tfeature space sparse and degrade clustering performance.\n387\tTherefore, we apply UMAP [26, 25] to map each feature vector from 902 dimensions to 5 dimensions. Let\n388\tϕUMAP : R902 → R5 be the UMAP mapping. Then, the\n389\tlow-dimensional representation for each keystroke sample\n390\tis ui = ϕUMAP (fi ) ∈ R5 , i = 1, 2, . . . , N , which is\n391\tused as the input to the hierarchical clustering step in Section 3.5.\n392\t\n393\tInteractive segmentation refinement. To verify the\n394\tcompleteness of keystroke timing detection and segmentation, we developed a lightweight GUI to inspect the waveform and the detected peaks, and to add, remove, or adjust peak positions before re-extracting segments from the\n395\tcorrected peak set P . The developed GUI is described in\n396\tAppendix B.\n397\t3.3 Feature Extraction\n398\tWe compute multiple acoustic features from each segmented keystroke waveform of 8,192 samples and construct a feature vector. First, for each segment si , we compute mel-frequency cepstral coefficients (MFCCs) [19], a\n399\tfeature representation widely used in speech recognition\n400\tand audio analysis. Zhuang et al. [43] reported that representing keystroke sounds with MFCCs achieves higher\n401\tinference accuracy than using a plain fast Fourier transform (FFT)-based representation alone. We set the FFT\n402\twindow length to 4,096, the hop length to 512, and the\n403\tnumber of Mel bands to 128. The MFCC features extracted from a segmented waveform si ∈ R8,192 are represented by MFCC(si ) ∈ R128×F , where F denotes\n404\tthe number of time frames. Let mi,j ∈ RF be the jth coefficient trajectory. We compute summary statistics\n405\tmax\n406\tmmean\n407\t, mvar\n408\t, mmin\n409\t, and mmed\n410\t∈ R128 , which\n411\ti\n412\ti , mi\n413\ti\n414\ti\n415\tcorrespond to the mean, variance, maximum, minimum,\n416\tand median over time for MFCC(si ), respectively. In addition, we define a spectral feature vector zi ∈ R6 that\n417\tconcatenates the mean and variance of the spectral centroid [20], spectral rolloff [21], and zero crossing rate [22].\n418\tWe also compute MFCC derivative features ∆i , ∆2i ∈\n419\tR128 . We then define the 902-dimensional feature vector\n420\t\n421\tUMAP is an algorithm that embeds data into a lowdimensional space while preserving local neighborhood\n422\tstructure in the original high-dimensional space. As a\n423\tresult, acoustically similar keystrokes are expected to be\n424\tplaced close to each other, while dissimilar keystrokes are\n425\tplaced farther apart. Although alternative dimensionality\n426\treduction methods such as principal component analysis\n427\t(PCA) [29] and t-distributed stochastic neighbor embedding (t-SNE) [24] exist, we adopt UMAP because it better preserves nonlinear structure while remaining computationally efficient.\n428\t3.5 Clustering\n429\tUsing the low-dimensional feature vectors obtained after\n430\tdimensionality reduction, ui ∈ R5 (i = 1, 2, . . . , N ),\n431\twe apply agglomerative clustering [37, 17, 16] to construct clusters of acoustically similar keystrokes that are\n432\tlikely to have been produced by the same key. We use\n433\tthe AgglomerativeClustering implementation in\n434\tscikit-learn.\n435\t4\n436\t\n437\t\fBecause UMAP places acoustically similar samples\n438\tclose to each other in the embedded space, hierarchical clustering enables us to assign acoustically similar\n439\tkeystroke sounds to the same cluster. In our experiments,\n440\twe fix the number of clusters to K = 25. Although the\n441\tcharacter set contains 29 symbols, using a smaller number\n442\tof clusters is more stable under limited observations (e.g.,\n443\t100–200 keystrokes). Empirically, we found that values\n444\tin the range of 15–30 provide robust performance, and we\n445\tuse K = 25 consistently across all experiments. After\n446\tclustering, the cluster index assigned to the i-th keystroke\n447\tsound sample is denoted by ci ∈ {1, 2, . . . , K}, and the\n448\tresulting cluster-index sequence is c = (c1 , c2 , . . . , cN ).\n449\t\n450\tNon-space keys\n451\t\n452\tf\n453\t\n454\te\n455\t\n456\ta\n457\t\n458\tb\n459\ts\n460\t\n461\td\n462\t\n463\tspace\n464\t\n465\tyh\n466\t\n467\tg\n468\tw\n469\tvu k n\n470\tp l m\n471\tperiod\n472\to comma\n473\ti\n474\tc\n475\t\n476\t(a) Ground-truth.\n477\t\n478\tIdentified space keys\n479\t\n480\t(b) Proposed method.\n481\t\n482\tFigure 4: 2-D visualization of keystroke distribution after\n483\tUMAP-based dimensionality reduction.\n484\t\n485\t3.6 Space-Key Identification\n486\t\n487\ta sentence. In this work, we focus on BERT’s standard\n488\tpretraining objective, masked language modeling (MLM),\n489\tand use it to infer typed text from a keystroke–cluster sequence. In MLM, the model predicts masked tokens in\n490\ta sentence; for example, given an input “i love [MASK]\n491\tpizza”, the model predicts a plausible token for [MASK]\n492\t(e.g., “eating”) based on the surrounding context. We estimate the typed text from the keystroke–cluster sequence\n493\tusing the character-level MLM task.\n494\t\n495\tThe space key often produces an acoustic signature that\n496\tis distinct from those of letter keys [43], because its size,\n497\tshape, and typing motion typically differ from those of\n498\tordinary letter keys. We exploit this property to identify\n499\tspace keystrokes in advance and use their positions as reliable anchors for the subsequent language-model-based\n500\tdecoding, which substantially improves stability.\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "1a58fbfd-7ed8-446d-8f23-9e5b1c2fa0fe", "uuid": "5628f749-36c1-4b75-a6cf-514737107866", "timestamp": "2026-07-27T05:50:15.645Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.txt", "content": "Transforming Keystroke Noise to Text:\nSelf-Supervised Acoustic Eavesdropping Attacks on Keyboards\nAtsunori Okada\n\nAkira Ito\n\nRei Ueno\n\nTohoku University\nokada.atsunori@dc.tohoku.ac.jp\n\nTohoku University\nakira.ito.b1@tohoku.ac.jp\n\nKyoto University\nueno.rei.2e@kyoto-u.ac.jp\n\nYuichi Hayashi\n\nNaofumi Homma\n\nNara Institute of Science and Technology\nyu-ichi@is.naist.jp\n\nTohoku University\nnaofumi.homma.c8@tohoku.ac.jp\n\narXiv:2607.22094v1 [cs.CR] 24 Jul 2026\n\nAbstract\nWe present a self-supervised acoustic eavesdropping attack that reconstructs typed text solely from keystroke sounds, without\nrequiring labeled data for the target device. The proposed attack enables stealthy eavesdropping in two real-world scenarios—physical spaces (public and semi-public) and online meetings. Our method combines unsupervised acoustic clustering\nwith Transformer-based language model inference and iterative self-training, enabling stable character inference under highly\nuncertain acoustic-to-character mappings. We demonstrate that the proposed method achieves over 99% reconstruction accuracy with only 100–150 observed keystrokes under a close-proximity recording setup using a smartphone placed near the\ntarget device, significantly outperforming prior unsupervised baselines in low-data regimes. We further evaluate robustness\nacross multiple laptop platforms and in realistic acquisition channels, including distance recording from approximately 3 meters away on the same desk, through-the-wall eavesdropping with a contact microphone, and background keyboard noise in\nonline conferencing systems. Across these scenarios, the proposed method achieves high reconstruction accuracy (often exceeding 90%) with approximately 150–250 observed keystrokes. These results indicate that accurate text reconstruction from\nkeystroke sounds is feasible in practice under an audio-only setting, even with limited observed keystrokes and without requiring device-specific labeled data, highlighting a realistic and previously underestimated privacy risk.\n\n1. Introduction\n\nHowever, supervised acoustic methods often depend on\nlabeled keystroke recordings collected in advance for the\ntarget device or a closely matched environment [2, 14, 3],\nwhich is difficult to obtain in realistic attack scenarios\nand can be sensitive to changes in the recording channel or keyboard (violating requirement (ii)). Unsupervised acoustic approaches remove the need for such profiling data, but they either rely on dedicated non-portable\nrecording setups, such as microphone arrays placed close\nto the keyboard [34], or require substantially more observed keystrokes to achieve stable inference [43], making them impractical under limited observations (violating\nrequirement (iii)). Electromagnetic (EM) attacks leverage signal leakage from wired keyboards, where digital\ninformation corresponding to keystrokes can be captured\nfrom emanations along cables [36]. However, such attacks typically require specialized sensing equipment and\ncareful measurement setups, limiting their practicality in\nreal-world scenarios (violating requirement (i)). Wireless\nsensing approaches, such as those based on WiFi channel state information (CSI) or beamforming feedback information (BFI), can infer typing behavior by analyzing\nsubtle changes in wireless signal strength caused by finger\nmovements [1, 18, 38, 15]. However, these approaches\noften rely on favorable transmitter/receiver placement or\naccess to wireless infrastructure, which may not be available to an attacker in realistic settings (violating requirement (i)). In addition, vision-based methods use cameras\nto capture typing motions and recover keystrokes from visual observations [40, 31, 5]. However, they typically require a clear line of sight to the victim, which significantly\nrestricts their applicability in everyday environments (violating requirement (i)). As a result, existing studies do not\nsimultaneously satisfy the aforementioned three requirements, which has been a major obstacle to practical key-\n\n1.1 Background\nRecovering typed text from keyboard input poses a serious\nthreat to user privacy and security, as it can expose sensitive information such as private communications, business discussions, and confidential documents. In modern\nworking environments, people increasingly type on laptops in public and semi-public spaces, including cafes, libraries, public transportation (e.g., bullet trains and airplanes), and waiting lounges, where their keystroke signals may unintentionally leak through physical side channels (e.g., keystroke sounds and electromagnetic radiation). Furthermore, online meetings have rapidly become\ncommon, and participants may type during calls while\ntheir keystroke sounds are captured by their microphones.\nThese daily scenarios make it possible for an adversary to\nobserve side-channel signals generated by typing without\nrequiring direct access to the device or privileged system\ninformation. Given this risk, an important question is what\nconstitutes a practical keystroke eavesdropping attack in\nreal-world conditions. In this paper, we consider such an\nattack to require three key properties: (i) minimal deployment constraints, (ii) no labeled data for the target device,\nand (iii) high reconstruction accuracy from only a limited\nnumber of observed keystrokes.\nWhile existing studies have demonstrated that keystroke\ninformation can be inferred from various side channels using different sensing modalities, these approaches do not\nfully satisfy the aforementioned requirements for practical attacks. Acoustic attacks exploit the fact that each key\nproduces a distinct sound pattern, enabling classification\nof keystrokes from audio recordings [2, 43, 14, 3, 4, 9].\n\n1\n\n\fboard eavesdropping attacks.\n\nMinimal\ndeployment\n(a) Public spaces\n\n1.2 Our Contribution\nTo address these limitations of existing approaches, we\npropose a self-supervised acoustic eavesdropping attack\ndesigned to satisfy all three requirements. Figure 1 provides an overview of the considered attack scenarios and\nhighlights the key properties of our approach. Our main\nidea is to leverage a Transformer-based language model\nto stabilize sequence prediction. Specifically, we employ\na character-level BERT model to infer the most plausible\ncharacter sequence from a noisy cluster sequence, using\nambiguity-aware embeddings that encode the probabilistic correspondences between clusters and characters. Because this method relies only on passively observed audio\nand does not require specialized sensing hardware, it can\noperate under minimal setup constraints (requirement (i)).\nMoreover, by utilizing linguistic priors instead of supervised acoustic-to-character mappings, the method eliminates the need for labeled data for the target device (requirement (ii)). Finally, by leveraging contextual inference\nwith a Transformer-based language model, namely BERT\nand LLM-based correction, the proposed method achieves\naccurate reconstruction from a limited number of observed\nkeystrokes, demonstrating strong performance in low-data\nregimes (requirement (iii)).\n\nNo labeled\ndata\n\n(c) Online meeting\n\nTyped text prediction\n\nAudio leakage\n\n(b) Through-wall\n\nHigh accuracy\nwith limited data\n\n…\n\ndear michael,\nthis is a quick\nfollow up regarding\nour recent discussion.\nafter reviewing the\nnotes with you…\n\nSelf-supervised\ninference\nmodel\n\nAttacker\n\nFigure 1: Example attack scenarios for acoustic keyboard\neavesdropping.\n\n2. Threat Model\n2.1 Adversarial Goals\nThe attacker’s goal is to reconstruct text typed by a victim\non a keyboard using only recorded keystroke sounds. We\nfocus primarily on English natural-language sentences,\nwhile noting that some security-critical data, including\npasswords and credit card numbers, are strings that do not\nfollow natural-language patterns. Accordingly, we primarily demonstrate eavesdropping attacks on natural-language\ntext and then evaluate random-string estimation as a proxy\nfor password recovery.\n\nTo validate the proposed attack, we evaluate the method\nthrough close-proximity recording, cross-device tests, and\nrealistic recording scenarios. In a simplified recording\nsetup, the proposed attack achieves more than 99% reconstruction accuracy using only about 100–150 keystrokes.\nWe further evaluate the method under practical acquisition conditions with minimal setup constraints, including distant recording on the same desk, through-the-wall\neavesdropping with a contact microphone, and audio captured through online conferencing systems. Experimental\nresults demonstrate that the proposed attack satisfies all\nthree requirements and that accurate text reconstruction\nfrom keystroke acoustics is practical in real-world environments.\n\n2.2 Victim Conditions\nWe assume a typical computer user typing English text\nduring a typing session. We characterize the victim as follows:\n• Character set. The victim types text composed of\nthe following 29 symbols: lowercase letters a--z\n(26 letters), space, comma (,), and period (.). For simplicity, the same character set assumption is used in\nthe random-password experiment in Section 6.2.\n\nThe main contributions of this paper are summarized as\nfollows:\n\n• Input device. The victim uses a standard physical\nkeyboard integrated into a laptop. In our evaluation,\nwe consider multiple laptop platforms to assess the\nrobustness of the proposed method across different\nkeyboard conditions. For more details, see Section 4\nand 5.\n\n• We design real-world keyboard eavesdropping attack\nscenarios and identify the requirements for practical\nattacks.\n\n• Typing speed. We assume a typing speed of 240\ncharacters per minute (CPM). Empirical studies report that typical users type at approximately 30–60\nwords per minute [8, 30], corresponding to about\n180–360 CPM assuming six characters per word.\nThis implies an average keystroke interval of roughly\n200–400 ms. At these speeds, consecutive keystroke\nsounds generally do not overlap, which is important\nbecause the proposed attack relies on audio waveform\nsegmentation. In other words, the proposed attack is\napplicable as long as consecutive keystroke sounds\ndo not overlap.\n\n• We propose a novel self-supervised inference\npipeline that achieves significantly higher accuracy\nthan prior unsupervised acoustic baselines across various settings.\n• We demonstrate the robustness and practicality of\nthe proposed approach across multiple devices and\nrealistic recording scenarios, including desk-surface\nrecording, through-the-wall acquisition, and online\nconferencing audio.\n\n2\n\n\f2.3 Adversarial Assumptions and Constraints\n\nclustering process converts keystroke sounds into a discrete cluster-index sequence, in which each cluster is expected to correspond to one or more possible characters\n(although the correspondence is initially unknown). Given\nan audio waveform of a typing session, we first detect\nkeystroke timings and segment the waveform into individual keystroke snippets (Section 3.2). From each snippet, we extract a high-dimensional acoustic feature vector\n(Section 3.3). We then apply UMAP-based dimensionality reduction to improve clustering reliability under limited observations (Sections 3.4 and 3.5). We explicitly\nidentify the space key using a distance threshold in the\ndimensionality-reduced space because its acoustic characteristics are often distinctive (Section 3.6); these space positions stabilize the subsequent language-model decoding.\nUsing a feedback learning loop, we then decode the cluster sequence into text by combining a cluster-to-character\nmapping with language-model priors. We define and initialize a probabilistic mapping matrix that represents the\ncorrespondence between clusters and characters and construct ambiguity-aware token embeddings from this matrix. A character-level BERT model then performs masked\nlanguage modeling over the embedding sequence to infer\nthe most likely character at each position (Section 3.7).\nWe further refine the decoded text using an LLM-based\ncontextual correction module (Section 3.8). To improve\nrobustness in low-data regimes, we incorporate iterative\nself-training. We extract high-confidence spans where the\nBERT output agrees with the LLM-corrected text, propagate pseudo-labels to the remaining keystrokes using label spreading in the acoustic feature space, and update\nthe emission matrix in the feedback loop (Sections 3.9\nand 3.10).\n\nThe attacker is assumed to observe the audio waveform\nproduced by the victim’s typing but to have no other information. The audio waveform is captured using an off-theshelf microphone. To model a practical attack, we assume\nthat the attacker operates under the following constraints:\n• Limited environmental control and portable\nrecording setup. Depending on the situation, the attacker may be able to place a microphone, but cannot arbitrarily change the victim’s keyboard or device configuration. Although the microphone may be\nplaced in a favorable position for low-noise recording, the attacker does not know its exact distance or\nangle from the target device.\n• No prior knowledge, no profiling, and no compromise of the target device. The attacker has no\ndetailed knowledge about what keyboard the victim\nuses and what type of English text the victim types.\nThe attacker obtains neither the ground-truth typed\ntext nor any labeled dataset that maps keys or characters to their corresponding acoustic signals. The\nattacker does not use malware such as keyloggers,\naccess OS-internal information, or abuse application\nprivileges on the victim device.\nThese constraints capture practical keyboard eavesdropping scenarios: opportunistic audio-only observation\nwithout any supervised data prepared in advance.\n2.4 Practical Settings and Proposed Scenarios\nIn this paper, we consider the following three microphone\nsetups: (a) a portable voice recorder, such as a smartphone, (b) a portable contact microphone, and (c) laptop built-in microphone. We set the audio sampling rate\nto 44.1 kHz, as is common in audio recording.\n\n3.2 Keystroke Segmentation\nWe segment a continuous audio recording into individual keystroke waveforms; that is, we detect the timing of\neach keystroke and extract a fixed-length window around\neach detected event. Figure 3 displays an example of a\nkeystroke audio waveform and its segmentation.\n\nWe consider three attack scenarios based on these microphone setups. Setups (a) and (b) enable distant\nrecording in (semi-)public spaces and through-the-wall\nrecording from an office or meeting room by placing the\nvoice recorder or contact microphone on a desk or wall, respectively. Setup (c) targets online meetings. In this scenario, the attacker obtains keystroke sounds as background\nnoise when the victim types during a meeting. Although\nsome online conferencing systems support noise cancellation, this feature may be disabled, unavailable, or provided\nonly as a paid feature. From an attacker’s perspective, such\nconditions are sufficient to exploit keystroke sounds leaked\nthrough meeting audio. This study demonstrates the feasibility of acoustic keyboard eavesdropping in these practical scenarios, raising a serious privacy concern.\n\nAutomatic keystroke detection. Given an audio waveform x(t), we compute its short-time Fourier transform\n(STFT) with a window length of 1,024 samples and track\nthe temporal change of the signal energy (i.e., sum of\nthe squared power spectral density). Figure 3 also shows\nthe short-window energy curve, which has a peak at\nthe position of a keystroke. Based on this observation,\nwe use a peak-detection algorithm that identifies peaks\nbased on prominence [6] to find candidate keystroke timings. Let P = {p1 , p2 , . . . , pN } be the detected peak\nindices, where N is the number of detected keystrokes.\nFor each pi , we extract a segment comprising consecutive L = 8,192 samples centered\u0001 at pi , i.e., si =\nxpi −L/2 , xpi −L/2+1 , . . . , xpi +L/2−1 . Keystroke sounds\noften contain both press and release components; we focus on the press components and align segments around\nthe press peak.\n\n3. Attack Design\n3.1 Overview\nFigure 2 presents the end-to-end pipeline of the proposed\nattack for reconstructing text from keystroke sounds. The\n3\n\n\fClustering process\nAudio\nwaveform\n\nKeystroke\nsegmentation\nSection 3.2\n\nFeature extraction\n（902-dim）\nSection 3.3\n\nDimension reduction\n（5-dim）\n\nClustering\n\nSection 3.4\n\nSection 3.5\n\nBERT\nprediction\nSection 3.7\n\nLLM-based\ncorrection\nSection 3.8\n\nClustering\nseries\n\nAcoustic features\n\nFeedback learning\nClustering\nseries\n\nSpace key\ndetection\nSection 3.6\n\nSelf-supervised\ntraining\nSection 3.9\n\nUpdate\nM-matrix\nSection 3.10\n\nLLM-based\ncorrection\n\nPredicted text\n\nFeedback loop\n\nFigure 2: Proposed attack pipeline.\nby concatenating all features as\n\u0002\nmax\nfi = mmean\n, mvar\n, mmin\n, mmed\n,\ni\ni , mi\ni\ni\n\u0003\n⊤\nzi , ∆i , ∆2i\n∈ R902 ,\n\nPush peak\n\nSegment\n\nRelease peak\n\nDetection point\n\n(1)\n\nwhich is the input to the subsequent dimensionality reduction stage. Note that we standardize the feature vectors\nacross keystroke sound samples before the dimensionality\nreduction.\n\nFigure 3: Example of audio segmentation using the waveform (top) and short-window energy curve (bottom).\n\n3.4 Dimensionality Reduction\nIn this work, we target eavesdropping attacks that operate\nwith only a limited number of observations (e.g., 100–200\nkeystrokes), where reliable inference must be achieved\nunder severe data constraints. In this regime, the feature dimensionality (902) is substantially larger than the\nnumber of observations. This imbalance may make the\nfeature space sparse and degrade clustering performance.\nTherefore, we apply UMAP [26, 25] to map each feature vector from 902 dimensions to 5 dimensions. Let\nϕUMAP : R902 → R5 be the UMAP mapping. Then, the\nlow-dimensional representation for each keystroke sample\nis ui = ϕUMAP (fi ) ∈ R5 , i = 1, 2, . . . , N , which is\nused as the input to the hierarchical clustering step in Section 3.5.\n\nInteractive segmentation refinement. To verify the\ncompleteness of keystroke timing detection and segmentation, we developed a lightweight GUI to inspect the waveform and the detected peaks, and to add, remove, or adjust peak positions before re-extracting segments from the\ncorrected peak set P . The developed GUI is described in\nAppendix B.\n3.3 Feature Extraction\nWe compute multiple acoustic features from each segmented keystroke waveform of 8,192 samples and construct a feature vector. First, for each segment si , we compute mel-frequency cepstral coefficients (MFCCs) [19], a\nfeature representation widely used in speech recognition\nand audio analysis. Zhuang et al. [43] reported that representing keystroke sounds with MFCCs achieves higher\ninference accuracy than using a plain fast Fourier transform (FFT)-based representation alone. We set the FFT\nwindow length to 4,096, the hop length to 512, and the\nnumber of Mel bands to 128. The MFCC features extracted from a segmented waveform si ∈ R8,192 are represented by MFCC(si ) ∈ R128×F , where F denotes\nthe number of time frames. Let mi,j ∈ RF be the jth coefficient trajectory. We compute summary statistics\nmax\nmmean\n, mvar\n, mmin\n, and mmed\n∈ R128 , which\ni\ni , mi\ni\ni\ncorrespond to the mean, variance, maximum, minimum,\nand median over time for MFCC(si ), respectively. In addition, we define a spectral feature vector zi ∈ R6 that\nconcatenates the mean and variance of the spectral centroid [20], spectral rolloff [21], and zero crossing rate [22].\nWe also compute MFCC derivative features ∆i , ∆2i ∈\nR128 . We then define the 902-dimensional feature vector\n\nUMAP is an algorithm that embeds data into a lowdimensional space while preserving local neighborhood\nstructure in the original high-dimensional space. As a\nresult, acoustically similar keystrokes are expected to be\nplaced close to each other, while dissimilar keystrokes are\nplaced farther apart. Although alternative dimensionality\nreduction methods such as principal component analysis\n(PCA) [29] and t-distributed stochastic neighbor embedding (t-SNE) [24] exist, we adopt UMAP because it better preserves nonlinear structure while remaining computationally efficient.\n3.5 Clustering\nUsing the low-dimensional feature vectors obtained after\ndimensionality reduction, ui ∈ R5 (i = 1, 2, . . . , N ),\nwe apply agglomerative clustering [37, 17, 16] to construct clusters of acoustically similar keystrokes that are\nlikely to have been produced by the same key. We use\nthe AgglomerativeClustering implementation in\nscikit-learn.\n4\n\n\fBecause UMAP places acoustically similar samples\nclose to each other in the embedded space, hierarchical clustering enables us to assign acoustically similar\nkeystroke sounds to the same cluster. In our experiments,\nwe fix the number of clusters to K = 25. Although the\ncharacter set contains 29 symbols, using a smaller number\nof clusters is more stable under limited observations (e.g.,\n100–200 keystrokes). Empirically, we found that values\nin the range of 15–30 provide robust performance, and we\nuse K = 25 consistently across all experiments. After\nclustering, the cluster index assigned to the i-th keystroke\nsound sample is denoted by ci ∈ {1, 2, . . . , K}, and the\nresulting cluster-index sequence is c = (c1 , c2 , . . . , cN ).\n\nNon-space keys\n\nf\n\ne\n\na\n\nb\ns\n\nd\n\nspace\n\nyh\n\ng\nw\nvu k n\np l m\nperiod\no comma\ni\nc\n\n(a) Ground-truth.\n\nIdentified space keys\n\n(b) Proposed method.\n\nFigure 4: 2-D visualization of keystroke distribution after\nUMAP-based dimensionality reduction.\n\n3.6 Space-Key Identification\n\na sentence. In this work, we focus on BERT’s standard\npretraining objective, masked language modeling (MLM),\nand use it to infer typed text from a keystroke–cluster sequence. In MLM, the model predicts masked tokens in\na sentence; for example, given an input “i love [MASK]\npizza”, the model predicts a plausible token for [MASK]\n(e.g., “eating”) based on the surrounding context. We estimate the typed text from the keystroke–cluster sequence\nusing the character-level MLM task.\n\nThe space key often produces an acoustic signature that\nis distinct from those of letter keys [43], because its size,\nshape, and typing motion typically differ from those of\nordinary letter keys. We exploit this property to identify\nspace keystrokes in advance and use their positions as reliable anchors for the subsequent language-model-based\ndecoding, which substantially improves stability.", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1, "totalLines": 1980}}}
