{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-bc23541f333c7393", "type": "tool_result", "content": "500\tdecoding, which substantially improves stability.\n501\tSeed selection. We first manually select a single spacekey sample by listening to the segmented keystroke\n502\tsounds. In practice, the space keystroke is easy to distinguish by ear in our recordings, and selecting one representative sample requires only minimal manual effort.\n503\t\n504\tTraining a character-level BERT To infer characters\n505\tcorresponding to keystroke sounds, we build a BERT\n506\tmodel that performs MLM at the character level. While\n507\tstandard pretrained BERT models assume word-piece tokenization, our setting uses a small character set consisting of lowercase letters a--z, space, comma (,), and period (.), for a total of 29 symbols. Therefore, we train a\n508\tcustom character-level BERT in which each character is\n509\ttreated as one token. For training data, we use a corpus\n510\tderived from OpenWebText [11], preprocessed to retain\n511\tonly the target symbols. The detailed training procedure\n512\tand hyperparameter settings of the BERT model are provided in Section D.\n513\t\n514\tDistance-based identification in the UMAP space. Let\n515\tui ∈ R5 denote the UMAP embedding of the i-th\n516\tkeystroke (Section 3.4). Given a manually selected seed\n517\tspace sample si⋆ with embedding ui⋆ , we identify all\n518\tkeystrokes whose Euclidean distance to the seed is within\n519\ta fixed threshold τspace = 2.0 as space keystrokes:\n520\tS = { i | ∥ui − ui⋆ ∥2 ≤ τspace } .\n521\t\n522\tt\n523\t\n524\tr\n525\t\n526\t(2)\n527\t\n528\tWe consider S as the set of space positions in the keystroke\n529\tsequence.\n530\t\n531\tProbabilistic cluster–character mapping. We define a\n532\tprobabilistic correspondence between clusters and characters by a matrix M ∈ [0, 1]|V |×K , where K is the number\n533\tof clusters and V is the vocabulary. The entry Mj,ci represents the probability that the cluster ci (assigned to the\n534\ti-th keystroke) corresponds to the j-th character v (j) ∈ V .\n535\tWe obtain an initial estimate M(0) using an HMM-based\n536\tprocedure with the expectation–maximization (EM) algorithm [43].\n537\t\n538\tVisualization. Figure 4 visualizes an example of the\n539\tkeystroke-sound distribution in a 2-D UMAP embedding,\n540\twhere Figure 4(a) shows the ground-truth key labels (for\n541\treference only and unavailable in actual attacks), and Figure 4(b) shows the result of the proposed method. In Figure 4(a), space keystroke sounds form a compact cluster\n542\tthat is well separated from non-space keystroke sounds.\n543\tAs a result, selecting a single seed sample and applying the\n544\tdistance threshold enables accurate identification of the remaining space keystrokes, as shown in Figure 4(b).\n545\t\n546\tConstructing\n547\tambiguity-aware\n548\tembeddings\n549\tTransformer-based models convert each input token\n550\tinto an embedding vector and process the embedding\n551\tsequence via attention. Let e(v (j) ) ∈ Rde denote the\n552\tembedding vector for character v (j) , where de is the\n553\tembedding dimension. Because a cluster may correspond to multiple characters probabilistically through\n554\tM, we represent this uncertainty as an ambiguity-aware\n555\tembedding. Specifically, we define the ambiguity-aware\n556\t\n557\t3.7 BERT-Based Text Prediction\n558\tBackground: BERT and masked language modeling.\n559\tBERT [7] is a bidirectional language model based on the\n560\tTransformer architecture [35]. A key property of BERT is\n561\tits ability to use bidirectional context: it can leverage both\n562\tleft and right contexts to infer the meaning of a token in\n563\t5\n564\t\n565\t\fembedding for the i-th keystroke as\n566\t\n567\thi =\n568\t\n569\t|V |\n570\tX\n571\t\n572\tMj,ci e(v\n573\t\n574\t3.8 LLM-Based Correction\n575\t(j)\n576\t\n577\t).\n578\t\n579\tThe character sequence ŷ predicted by BERT can still contain errors due to inaccuracies in the estimated cluster–\n580\tcharacter mapping matrix M and uncertainty in BERT’s\n581\tcontextual interpretation. To further improve the contextual coherence of the reconstruction, we utilize an LLM to\n582\tpost-edit the BERT output.\n583\t\n584\t(3)\n585\t\n586\tj=1\n587\t\n588\tThis weighted sum captures the uncertainty in which multiple characters are plausible for a cluster. By applying\n589\tEquation (3) to all positions, we obtain an embedding sequence H = (h1 , h2 , . . . , hN ).\n590\t\n591\tLet LLM(·) denote the LLM-based correction function. Given a BERT prediction ŷ, the corrected string\n592\tis obtained as y∗ = LLM(ŷ). While BERT performs\n593\tcharacter prediction mainly based on local bidirectional\n594\tcontext, an LLM can leverage longer-range dependencies over the entire sentence and produce a grammatically and semantically consistent string. In our implementation, we use Google Gemini 2.0 Flash (version:\n595\tgemini-2.0-flash-001) to correct contextual errors\n596\tin the BERT output, thereby achieving higher reconstruction accuracy. The prompts used for the LLM-based correction are provided in the Appendix E.\n597\t\n598\tCharacter prediction with MLM We apply\n599\tMLM to the embedding sequence H to predict\n600\tcharacters.\n601\tFor each position i, we create an\n602\tinput sequence where the ambiguity-aware embedding at position i is replaced by [MASK]:\n603\tH(i)\n604\t=\n605\t(h1 , h2 , . . . , hi−1 , e[MASK] , hi+1 , . . . , hN ).\n606\tThe predicted character is then given by\n607\t\u0011\n608\t\u0010\n609\tŷi = arg max PBERT v (j) | H(i) .\n610\tv (j) ∈V\n611\t\n612\t(4)\n613\t\n614\t3.9 Self-Training with Label Spreading\n615\t\n616\tWe repeat this procedure for all positions i = 1, 2, . . . , N\n617\tto obtain the final predicted character sequence ŷ =\n618\t(ŷ1 , ŷ2 , . . . , ŷN ). Because BERT can exploit bidirectional\n619\tcontext, it can resolve ambiguities where a single cluster may correspond to multiple characters and thereby improve prediction accuracy.\n620\t\n621\tConfidence-based self-labeling The BERT-predicted\n622\tcharacter sequence ŷ may contain errors, and the LLMcorrected sequence y∗ may also introduce edits that are\n623\tnot perfectly aligned with the underlying keystroke sequence. To obtain reliable pseudo labels, we extract only\n624\thigh-confidence spans based on the agreement between ŷ\n625\tand y∗ . We first split both strings into word sequences by\n626\twhitespace and perform a diff-based alignment to obtain\n627\ta set of corresponding phrase pairs C = {(om , cm )}M\n628\tm=1 ,\n629\twhere om and cm denote a phrase before and after correction, respectively. For each pair, we compute a normalized edit similarity sim(om , cm ) ∈ [0, 1] and regard it as a\n630\thigh-confidence phrase if it satisfies sim(om , cm ) ≥ τsim\n631\tand |om | = |cm | (with τsim = 0.6 in our experiments).\n632\tFor each high-confidence phrase, we replace om with cm\n633\tin ŷ to obtain a pseudo-labeled string ỹ. We also assign\n634\ta confidence mask mi ∈ {0, 1} to each character position i, where mi = 1 indicates that the position is covered by a high-confidence phrase and is treated as pseudolabeled. The resulting pair (ỹ, m) is used as input to the\n635\tsemi-supervised refinement described next.\n636\t\n637\tMultiple trials for BERT initialization. The accuracy of the initial BERT decoding can vary substantially depending on the initial clustering results. Because\n638\tUMAP involves random initialization, the resulting lowdimensional representation and the subsequent clustering\n639\tcan differ across runs. To reduce this instability, we repeat UMAP-based dimensionality reduction and clustering multiple times and use the best result as the initial state\n640\tfor BERT decoding. In our experiments, we perform this\n641\t(r)\n642\t(r)\n643\tprocedure R = 10 times. Let ŷ(r) = (ŷ1 , . . . , ŷN ) denote the decoded character sequence obtained from the r(r)\n644\tth UMAP-and-clustering trial, and let PBERT,i (v) be the\n645\tBERT posterior probability of character v at position i\n646\tunder that trial. Because the attacker does not know the\n647\tground-truth text, the trials cannot be compared by reconstruction accuracy. Instead, we evaluate each trial by the\n648\taverage log-likelihood of its own decoded sequence:\n649\t\n650\tSemi-supervised refinement via label spreading Using the pseudo labels obtained above, we refine the remaining unlabeled positions by applying label spreading,\n651\ta semi-supervised learning method. In our setting, the\n652\tnumber of observed keystrokes is only on the order of\n653\t100–200, so the amount of pseudo-labeled data can be\n654\tlimited; under such conditions, training a fully supervised\n655\tclassifier often yields high variance and poor generalization. Label spreading instead leverages both labeled and\n656\tunlabeled samples by propagating label information over\n657\ta similarity graph in feature space. For each keystroke index i, let ui ∈ R5 denote the low-dimensional feature vector obtained by UMAP, and let ỹi be its pseudo-character\n658\tlabel when available. Based on the confidence mask mi ,\n659\twe partition the samples into a labeled set (mi = 1) and\n660\t\n661\tN\n662\t\n663\tL(r) =\n664\t\n665\t\u0010\n666\t\u0011\n667\t1 X\n668\t(r)\n669\t(r)\n670\t.\n671\tlog PBERT,i ŷi\n672\tN i=1\n673\t\n674\t(5)\n675\t\n676\tWe then select the trial\n677\tr⋆ = arg max L(r) ,\n678\t\n679\t(6)\n680\t\n681\tr∈{1,2,...,R}\n682\t\n683\tand use the corresponding clustering result and decoded\n684\tsequence as the initial state for the subsequent feedback\n685\tloop. This procedure chooses the most likely initialization under the BERT model itself, without requiring any\n686\tlabeled text from the target.\n687\t6\n688\t\n689\t\fan unlabeled set (mi = 0). Starting from the labeled set\n690\t{(uj , ỹj ) | mj = 1}, we apply label spreading to infer\n691\tcharacter labels for all samples based on their similarity\n692\tin the feature space: ȳi = LabelSpread(ui ). Unlike hard\n693\tself-labeling, label spreading allows labels—including initially assigned pseudo-labels—to change during propagation when the local neighborhood structure supports a different label. This flexibility helps the refinement process\n694\tavoid poor local optima caused by early mistakes in ỹ. By\n695\tapplying label spreading to all positions, we obtain a refined character sequence ȳ = (ȳ1 , ȳ2 , . . . , ȳN ), which is\n696\tused in the subsequent feedback learning stage.\n697\t\n698\tThe Levenshtein distance is the minimum number of character edit operations (insertions, deletions, and substitutions) required to transform one string into another, and\n699\tthus measures the dissimilarity between two strings. Given\n700\tpredicted and ground-truth strings spred and sorig , we evaluate the reconstruct accuracy using the score defined as\n701\tfollows:\n702\t\u0013\n703\t\u0012\n704\td(spred , sorig )\n705\t×100[%],\n706\trlev (spred , sorig ) = 1 −\n707\tmax(|spred |, |sorig |)\n708\t(8)\n709\twhere |spred | and |sorig | denote the string lengths in characters, and d(spred , sorig ) denotes the Levenshtein distance.\n710\t\n711\t3.10 Iterative Feedback\n712\t4.2 Accuracy for Limited Keystrokes\n713\t\n714\tUsing the refined character sequence obtained after selftraining and the cluster sequence c, we re-estimate a\n715\tcluster–character correspondence matrix M′ . We then update the current matrix M in a gradual manner using a\n716\tmixing parameter β. The update rule is given by\n717\tMnew = (1 − β) M + β M′ .\n718\t\n719\tThis subsection demonstrates experimentally that the proposed method can reconstruct text with substantially fewer\n720\tobserved keystrokes than existing unsupervised baselines\n721\tunder a simplified setting. We compare the proposed\n722\tBERT-based pipeline with two existing approaches: the\n723\tHMM-based method with feedback learning [43] and\n724\ta dictionary-based method [9]. The dictionary-based\n725\tmethod implemented in this work follows prior research\n726\ton dictionary-constrained keystroke inference. Early work\n727\tby Berger et al. [4] in ACM CCS 2006 introduced dictionary attacks based on acoustic similarity constraints between keys. More recent works, such as Yang et al. [38],\n728\tproposed a training-free dictionary demodulation framework that exploits structural patterns (i.e., repetition relationships) in symbol sequences. This framework has\n729\tbeen further adapted to acoustic side-channel settings [9],\n730\tdemonstrating its applicability beyond wireless signals. In\n731\tthis work, we implement the structure-based dictionary\n732\tmethod [9], in which cluster sequences are matched to\n733\tcandidate words using repetition patterns and consistent\n734\tcluster-to-character mappings are enforced across words.\n735\tFor a fair comparison, we additionally apply the same\n736\tLLM-based post-processing (Gemini) used in our method\n737\tto the output of the dictionary-based approach.\n738\t\n739\t(7)\n740\t\n741\tHere, β ∈ (0, 1) is an update weight analogous to a learning rate, and we set β = 0.8 in our experiments.\n742\tUsing the updated Mnew , we rerun BERT inference,\n743\tLLM-based correction, and self-training, and repeat this\n744\tfeedback loop multiple times. Through this iterative process, the acoustic model (cluster–character mapping) and\n745\tthe contextual models (BERT/LLM) complement each\n746\tother and progressively improve, substantially increasing\n747\tthe final reconstruction accuracy. In our implementation,\n748\twe use Tmax = 50 iterations. We empirically found that\n749\t50 iterations were sufficient for stable convergence; therefore, we use this fixed value consistently across all experiments.\n750\t\n751\t4. Experiments under Simplified Conditions\n752\tThis section first describes a simple eavesdropping setting\n753\tand then presents experimental results as a proof of concept for our method.\n754\t\n755\tWe use the recording setup described in Section 4.1.\n756\tThe victim types a long English passage containing 2,444\n757\tcharacters, shown in Appendix C, and we run inference\n758\tusing only the first n keystrokes while varying n from 50\n759\tto 2,400 keystrokes. For the HMM-based and proposed\n760\tBERT-based methods, we record both the pre-feedback\n761\tand post-feedback accuracy. For the dictionary-based\n762\tmethod, we report the raw decoding result and the result after LLM-based correction. We report reconstruction\n763\tperformance using the Levenshtein score defined in Section 4.1.\n764\t\n765\t4.1 Experimental Setup\n766\tRecording setup. Figure 5 shows the recording setup\n767\tused throughout this section. The victim types on the keyboard of a 13-inch MacBook Pro (2019) keyboard while a\n768\tsmartphone (Apple iPhone 15) is placed beside the target\n769\tPC to record the keystroke sounds. We use this nearbyrecording condition as a simple and attacker-favorable\n770\tsetup in order to evaluate the proposed method under simplified conditions before moving to more realistic scenarios in Section 5.\n771\t\n772\tFigure 6 reports the experimental results. Figure 6(a)\n773\tshows the full range from 50 to 2,400 keystrokes, and\n774\tFigure 6(b) enlarges the low-data regime from 50 to 400\n775\tkeystrokes. For each method, dashed lines indicate the\n776\taccuracy before correction and solid lines indicate the accuracy after correction; the lightly shaded region represents the improvement obtained by feedback learning or\n777\tLLM correction. The proposed method reaches 99.00%\n778\taccuracy with only 100 observed keystrokes, correspond-\n779\t\n780\tEvaluation metrics. We evaluate reconstruction performance using the Levenshtein score, defined as a normalized complement of the edit distance (Levenshtein distance), where higher values indicate better reconstruction.\n781\t7\n782\t\n783\t\f100\n784\tLevenshtein score [%]\n785\t\n786\tTarget PC\n787\t\n788\tSmartphone\n789\t\n790\t80\n791\t60\n792\tDell Latitude 7320\n793\tApple MacBook Pro (2019)\n794\tHP OmniBook X14\n795\tLenovo ThinkPad X390\n796\t\n797\t40\n798\t20\n799\t0 50\n800\t\n801\t100\n802\t\n803\t150\n804\t\n805\t200\n806\t250\n807\t300\n808\tNumber of keystrokes\n809\t\n810\t350\n811\t\n812\t400\n813\t\n814\tFigure 7: Experimental results on four laptops recorded by\n815\ta smartphone.\n816\t\n817\t100\n818\t\n819\t100\n820\t\n821\t80\n822\t\n823\t80\n824\t\n825\t60\n826\t40\n827\tThis work\n828\tHMM-based\n829\tDictionary-based\n830\t\n831\t20\n832\t0 0\n833\t\n834\t500\n835\t\n836\t1000 1500 2000\n837\tNumber of keystrokes\n838\t\n839\t2500\n840\t\n841\t(a) Full range (50–2,400 keys).\n842\t\n843\tLevenshtein score [%]\n844\t\n845\tLevenshtein score [%]\n846\t\n847\tFigure 5: Experimental setup using a 13-inch MacBook\n848\tPro (2019) and a smartphone.\n849\t\n850\tpendix C (426, 426, and 438 characters, respectively), and\n851\twe recorded the keystroke sounds using the same setup as\n852\tin Section 4.1.\n853\tFor each recording, we ran reconstruction using only the\n854\tfirst n keystrokes, where n ranges from 50 to 400 in increments of 50. We compute the Levenshtein score for each\n855\trun. Figure 7 reports the mean score across the three texts.\n856\tOverall, our method achieves consistently high reconstruction accuracy across all four platforms once at least 150\n857\tkeystrokes are available. This result confirms the robust\n858\tperformance of the proposed eavesdropping attack.\n859\t\n860\t60\n861\t40\n862\t20\n863\t0\n864\t\n865\t100\n866\t\n867\t200\n868\t300\n869\tNumber of keystrokes\n870\t\n871\t(b) Low-data\n872\tkeys).\n873\t\n874\tregime\n875\t\n876\t400\n877\t\n878\t(50–400\n879\t\n880\tFigure 6: Evaluation results on the MacBook Pro.\n881\t\n882\t5. Validation in Real-World Scenarios\n883\ting to 17 words. At this point, the pre-feedback accuracy\n884\tis 47.00%, so feedback learning improves the score by\n885\t52.00 percentage points. With 150 observed keystrokes,\n886\tcorresponding to 23 words, the proposed method reaches\n887\t99.33% accuracy, while the pre-feedback accuracy is\n888\t62.67%, yielding an additional gain of 36.66 percentage\n889\tpoints.\n890\t\n891\tIn this section, we examine our attack in the three realworld scenarios proposed in Section 2: distant recording\n892\ton the same desk (Section 5.1), through-wall recording\n893\t(Section 5.2), and online meetings (Section 5.3). We use\n894\tthe same four laptop platforms and the same three texts as\n895\tin Section 4.3.\n896\t\n897\tThe HMM-based method improves much more slowly\n898\tand reaches 97.27% only at about 1,200 observed\n899\tkeystrokes, corresponding to approximately 178 words.\n900\tBeyond that point, its accuracy remains roughly unchanged. The dictionary-based method reaches 97.13% at\n901\t350 observed keystrokes and thereafter fluctuates slightly\n902\twhile remaining mostly above 90%. In the text used in this\n903\texperiment, 350 characters correspond to approximately\n904\t53 words, which is close to the 50-word setting reported\n905\tby Yang et al. [38]. Although Yang et al. studied a WiFibased attack, this result indicates that a similar dictionarybased decoding effect can also be reproduced in the acoustic setting as well. Overall, the proposed method achieves\n906\thigh-accuracy reconstruction with substantially fewer observed characters and words than both the HMM-based\n907\tand dictionary-based baselines.\n908\t\n909\tThe contact microphone used in the experiments in Section 5.1 and Section 5.2 captures structure-borne vibrations from a rigid object (here, the desk surface or wall)\n910\trather than airborne sound. Because vibrations can propagate over long distances within the same rigid body,\n911\tkeystroke information can remain more observable even\n912\tif the contact microphone is placed farther from the target, when compared with an ordinary voice recorder. This\n913\tsensing modality also has two practical advantages for an\n914\tattacker: (i) it is less affected by ambient acoustic noise\n915\tcompared than voice recorders, and (ii) the contact microphone is small and easy to conceal in public and shared\n916\tspaces (e.g., libraries and meeting rooms) by attaching it\n917\tunobtrusively to a desk surface. These properties make\n918\tcontact-microphone-based eavesdropping practical and realistic. We used an FL-1000 from Sun-Mechatronics [32]\n919\tin our experiments.\n920\t\n921\t4.3 Evaluation on Multiple Laptop Platforms\n922\t\n923\t5.1 Distant Recording on Same Desk\n924\t\n925\tWe then evaluate the robustness of our attack to differences in keyboard and chassis design by conducting experiments on four off-the-shelf laptops: a Dell Latitude\n926\t7320, a 13-inch MacBook Pro (2019), an HP OmniBook\n927\tX 14, and a Lenovo ThinkPad X390. For each laptop, the\n928\tvictim typed three different English passages shown in Ap-\n929\t\n930\tFigure 8(a) shows an overview of the experimental setup,\n931\tin which the attacker is 3.0 m away from the victim on the\n932\tsame desk surface. Figure 8(b) shows the attacker’s equipment: comprising a contact microphone, an audio amplifier, and a laptop used to record the signal. Although the\n933\tattacker is depicted using a laptop for recording in Fig8\n934\t\n935\t\fVictim’s PC\n936\t\n937\tWall (plaster)\n938\t\n939\tAttacker’s PC\n940\t\n941\tVictim’s room\n942\t\n943\tContact microphone\n944\t\n945\tAttacker’s PC\n946\t\n947\tContact microphone\n948\t\n949\t3.0 m\n950\t\n951\tTarget PC\n952\t\n953\tContact microphone\n954\t\n955\t(a) Experimental setup.\n956\t\n957\tPC for recording\n958\t\n959\tAudio amplifier\n960\t\n961\t(b) Attacker’s equipment.\n962\t\n963\tFigure 8: Overview of distant keystroke recording on desk.\n964\t\n965\t(a) Attack concept.\n966\t\n967\t100\n968\tLevenshtein score [%]\n969\t\n970\tAttacker’s room\n971\t\n972\tAttacker’s PC\n973\t\n974\tContact mic\n975\t\n976\t80\n977\t60\n978\tDell Latitude 7320\n979\tApple MacBook Pro (2019)\n980\tHP OmniBook X14\n981\tLenovo ThinkPad X390\n982\t\n983\t40\n984\t20\n985\t0 50\n986\t\n987\t100\n988\t\n989\t150\n990\t\n991\t200\n992\t250\n993\t300\n994\tNumber of keystrokes\n995\t\n996\t350\n997\t\n998\tAmplifier\n999\t\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "1a58fbfd-7ed8-446d-8f23-9e5b1c2fa0fe", "uuid": "f1ec3a28-9ac5-4b16-82b9-cffd851661a0", "timestamp": "2026-07-27T05:50:22.554Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.txt", "content": "decoding, which substantially improves stability.\nSeed selection. We first manually select a single spacekey sample by listening to the segmented keystroke\nsounds. In practice, the space keystroke is easy to distinguish by ear in our recordings, and selecting one representative sample requires only minimal manual effort.\n\nTraining a character-level BERT To infer characters\ncorresponding to keystroke sounds, we build a BERT\nmodel that performs MLM at the character level. While\nstandard pretrained BERT models assume word-piece tokenization, our setting uses a small character set consisting of lowercase letters a--z, space, comma (,), and period (.), for a total of 29 symbols. Therefore, we train a\ncustom character-level BERT in which each character is\ntreated as one token. For training data, we use a corpus\nderived from OpenWebText [11], preprocessed to retain\nonly the target symbols. The detailed training procedure\nand hyperparameter settings of the BERT model are provided in Section D.\n\nDistance-based identification in the UMAP space. Let\nui ∈ R5 denote the UMAP embedding of the i-th\nkeystroke (Section 3.4). Given a manually selected seed\nspace sample si⋆ with embedding ui⋆ , we identify all\nkeystrokes whose Euclidean distance to the seed is within\na fixed threshold τspace = 2.0 as space keystrokes:\nS = { i | ∥ui − ui⋆ ∥2 ≤ τspace } .\n\nt\n\nr\n\n(2)\n\nWe consider S as the set of space positions in the keystroke\nsequence.\n\nProbabilistic cluster–character mapping. We define a\nprobabilistic correspondence between clusters and characters by a matrix M ∈ [0, 1]|V |×K , where K is the number\nof clusters and V is the vocabulary. The entry Mj,ci represents the probability that the cluster ci (assigned to the\ni-th keystroke) corresponds to the j-th character v (j) ∈ V .\nWe obtain an initial estimate M(0) using an HMM-based\nprocedure with the expectation–maximization (EM) algorithm [43].\n\nVisualization. Figure 4 visualizes an example of the\nkeystroke-sound distribution in a 2-D UMAP embedding,\nwhere Figure 4(a) shows the ground-truth key labels (for\nreference only and unavailable in actual attacks), and Figure 4(b) shows the result of the proposed method. In Figure 4(a), space keystroke sounds form a compact cluster\nthat is well separated from non-space keystroke sounds.\nAs a result, selecting a single seed sample and applying the\ndistance threshold enables accurate identification of the remaining space keystrokes, as shown in Figure 4(b).\n\nConstructing\nambiguity-aware\nembeddings\nTransformer-based models convert each input token\ninto an embedding vector and process the embedding\nsequence via attention. Let e(v (j) ) ∈ Rde denote the\nembedding vector for character v (j) , where de is the\nembedding dimension. Because a cluster may correspond to multiple characters probabilistically through\nM, we represent this uncertainty as an ambiguity-aware\nembedding. Specifically, we define the ambiguity-aware\n\n3.7 BERT-Based Text Prediction\nBackground: BERT and masked language modeling.\nBERT [7] is a bidirectional language model based on the\nTransformer architecture [35]. A key property of BERT is\nits ability to use bidirectional context: it can leverage both\nleft and right contexts to infer the meaning of a token in\n5\n\n\fembedding for the i-th keystroke as\n\nhi =\n\n|V |\nX\n\nMj,ci e(v\n\n3.8 LLM-Based Correction\n(j)\n\n).\n\nThe character sequence ŷ predicted by BERT can still contain errors due to inaccuracies in the estimated cluster–\ncharacter mapping matrix M and uncertainty in BERT’s\ncontextual interpretation. To further improve the contextual coherence of the reconstruction, we utilize an LLM to\npost-edit the BERT output.\n\n(3)\n\nj=1\n\nThis weighted sum captures the uncertainty in which multiple characters are plausible for a cluster. By applying\nEquation (3) to all positions, we obtain an embedding sequence H = (h1 , h2 , . . . , hN ).\n\nLet LLM(·) denote the LLM-based correction function. Given a BERT prediction ŷ, the corrected string\nis obtained as y∗ = LLM(ŷ). While BERT performs\ncharacter prediction mainly based on local bidirectional\ncontext, an LLM can leverage longer-range dependencies over the entire sentence and produce a grammatically and semantically consistent string. In our implementation, we use Google Gemini 2.0 Flash (version:\ngemini-2.0-flash-001) to correct contextual errors\nin the BERT output, thereby achieving higher reconstruction accuracy. The prompts used for the LLM-based correction are provided in the Appendix E.\n\nCharacter prediction with MLM We apply\nMLM to the embedding sequence H to predict\ncharacters.\nFor each position i, we create an\ninput sequence where the ambiguity-aware embedding at position i is replaced by [MASK]:\nH(i)\n=\n(h1 , h2 , . . . , hi−1 , e[MASK] , hi+1 , . . . , hN ).\nThe predicted character is then given by\n\u0011\n\u0010\nŷi = arg max PBERT v (j) | H(i) .\nv (j) ∈V\n\n(4)\n\n3.9 Self-Training with Label Spreading\n\nWe repeat this procedure for all positions i = 1, 2, . . . , N\nto obtain the final predicted character sequence ŷ =\n(ŷ1 , ŷ2 , . . . , ŷN ). Because BERT can exploit bidirectional\ncontext, it can resolve ambiguities where a single cluster may correspond to multiple characters and thereby improve prediction accuracy.\n\nConfidence-based self-labeling The BERT-predicted\ncharacter sequence ŷ may contain errors, and the LLMcorrected sequence y∗ may also introduce edits that are\nnot perfectly aligned with the underlying keystroke sequence. To obtain reliable pseudo labels, we extract only\nhigh-confidence spans based on the agreement between ŷ\nand y∗ . We first split both strings into word sequences by\nwhitespace and perform a diff-based alignment to obtain\na set of corresponding phrase pairs C = {(om , cm )}M\nm=1 ,\nwhere om and cm denote a phrase before and after correction, respectively. For each pair, we compute a normalized edit similarity sim(om , cm ) ∈ [0, 1] and regard it as a\nhigh-confidence phrase if it satisfies sim(om , cm ) ≥ τsim\nand |om | = |cm | (with τsim = 0.6 in our experiments).\nFor each high-confidence phrase, we replace om with cm\nin ŷ to obtain a pseudo-labeled string ỹ. We also assign\na confidence mask mi ∈ {0, 1} to each character position i, where mi = 1 indicates that the position is covered by a high-confidence phrase and is treated as pseudolabeled. The resulting pair (ỹ, m) is used as input to the\nsemi-supervised refinement described next.\n\nMultiple trials for BERT initialization. The accuracy of the initial BERT decoding can vary substantially depending on the initial clustering results. Because\nUMAP involves random initialization, the resulting lowdimensional representation and the subsequent clustering\ncan differ across runs. To reduce this instability, we repeat UMAP-based dimensionality reduction and clustering multiple times and use the best result as the initial state\nfor BERT decoding. In our experiments, we perform this\n(r)\n(r)\nprocedure R = 10 times. Let ŷ(r) = (ŷ1 , . . . , ŷN ) denote the decoded character sequence obtained from the r(r)\nth UMAP-and-clustering trial, and let PBERT,i (v) be the\nBERT posterior probability of character v at position i\nunder that trial. Because the attacker does not know the\nground-truth text, the trials cannot be compared by reconstruction accuracy. Instead, we evaluate each trial by the\naverage log-likelihood of its own decoded sequence:\n\nSemi-supervised refinement via label spreading Using the pseudo labels obtained above, we refine the remaining unlabeled positions by applying label spreading,\na semi-supervised learning method. In our setting, the\nnumber of observed keystrokes is only on the order of\n100–200, so the amount of pseudo-labeled data can be\nlimited; under such conditions, training a fully supervised\nclassifier often yields high variance and poor generalization. Label spreading instead leverages both labeled and\nunlabeled samples by propagating label information over\na similarity graph in feature space. For each keystroke index i, let ui ∈ R5 denote the low-dimensional feature vector obtained by UMAP, and let ỹi be its pseudo-character\nlabel when available. Based on the confidence mask mi ,\nwe partition the samples into a labeled set (mi = 1) and\n\nN\n\nL(r) =\n\n\u0010\n\u0011\n1 X\n(r)\n(r)\n.\nlog PBERT,i ŷi\nN i=1\n\n(5)\n\nWe then select the trial\nr⋆ = arg max L(r) ,\n\n(6)\n\nr∈{1,2,...,R}\n\nand use the corresponding clustering result and decoded\nsequence as the initial state for the subsequent feedback\nloop. This procedure chooses the most likely initialization under the BERT model itself, without requiring any\nlabeled text from the target.\n6\n\n\fan unlabeled set (mi = 0). Starting from the labeled set\n{(uj , ỹj ) | mj = 1}, we apply label spreading to infer\ncharacter labels for all samples based on their similarity\nin the feature space: ȳi = LabelSpread(ui ). Unlike hard\nself-labeling, label spreading allows labels—including initially assigned pseudo-labels—to change during propagation when the local neighborhood structure supports a different label. This flexibility helps the refinement process\navoid poor local optima caused by early mistakes in ỹ. By\napplying label spreading to all positions, we obtain a refined character sequence ȳ = (ȳ1 , ȳ2 , . . . , ȳN ), which is\nused in the subsequent feedback learning stage.\n\nThe Levenshtein distance is the minimum number of character edit operations (insertions, deletions, and substitutions) required to transform one string into another, and\nthus measures the dissimilarity between two strings. Given\npredicted and ground-truth strings spred and sorig , we evaluate the reconstruct accuracy using the score defined as\nfollows:\n\u0013\n\u0012\nd(spred , sorig )\n×100[%],\nrlev (spred , sorig ) = 1 −\nmax(|spred |, |sorig |)\n(8)\nwhere |spred | and |sorig | denote the string lengths in characters, and d(spred , sorig ) denotes the Levenshtein distance.\n\n3.10 Iterative Feedback\n4.2 Accuracy for Limited Keystrokes\n\nUsing the refined character sequence obtained after selftraining and the cluster sequence c, we re-estimate a\ncluster–character correspondence matrix M′ . We then update the current matrix M in a gradual manner using a\nmixing parameter β. The update rule is given by\nMnew = (1 − β) M + β M′ .\n\nThis subsection demonstrates experimentally that the proposed method can reconstruct text with substantially fewer\nobserved keystrokes than existing unsupervised baselines\nunder a simplified setting. We compare the proposed\nBERT-based pipeline with two existing approaches: the\nHMM-based method with feedback learning [43] and\na dictionary-based method [9]. The dictionary-based\nmethod implemented in this work follows prior research\non dictionary-constrained keystroke inference. Early work\nby Berger et al. [4] in ACM CCS 2006 introduced dictionary attacks based on acoustic similarity constraints between keys. More recent works, such as Yang et al. [38],\nproposed a training-free dictionary demodulation framework that exploits structural patterns (i.e., repetition relationships) in symbol sequences. This framework has\nbeen further adapted to acoustic side-channel settings [9],\ndemonstrating its applicability beyond wireless signals. In\nthis work, we implement the structure-based dictionary\nmethod [9], in which cluster sequences are matched to\ncandidate words using repetition patterns and consistent\ncluster-to-character mappings are enforced across words.\nFor a fair comparison, we additionally apply the same\nLLM-based post-processing (Gemini) used in our method\nto the output of the dictionary-based approach.\n\n(7)\n\nHere, β ∈ (0, 1) is an update weight analogous to a learning rate, and we set β = 0.8 in our experiments.\nUsing the updated Mnew , we rerun BERT inference,\nLLM-based correction, and self-training, and repeat this\nfeedback loop multiple times. Through this iterative process, the acoustic model (cluster–character mapping) and\nthe contextual models (BERT/LLM) complement each\nother and progressively improve, substantially increasing\nthe final reconstruction accuracy. In our implementation,\nwe use Tmax = 50 iterations. We empirically found that\n50 iterations were sufficient for stable convergence; therefore, we use this fixed value consistently across all experiments.\n\n4. Experiments under Simplified Conditions\nThis section first describes a simple eavesdropping setting\nand then presents experimental results as a proof of concept for our method.\n\nWe use the recording setup described in Section 4.1.\nThe victim types a long English passage containing 2,444\ncharacters, shown in Appendix C, and we run inference\nusing only the first n keystrokes while varying n from 50\nto 2,400 keystrokes. For the HMM-based and proposed\nBERT-based methods, we record both the pre-feedback\nand post-feedback accuracy. For the dictionary-based\nmethod, we report the raw decoding result and the result after LLM-based correction. We report reconstruction\nperformance using the Levenshtein score defined in Section 4.1.\n\n4.1 Experimental Setup\nRecording setup. Figure 5 shows the recording setup\nused throughout this section. The victim types on the keyboard of a 13-inch MacBook Pro (2019) keyboard while a\nsmartphone (Apple iPhone 15) is placed beside the target\nPC to record the keystroke sounds. We use this nearbyrecording condition as a simple and attacker-favorable\nsetup in order to evaluate the proposed method under simplified conditions before moving to more realistic scenarios in Section 5.\n\nFigure 6 reports the experimental results. Figure 6(a)\nshows the full range from 50 to 2,400 keystrokes, and\nFigure 6(b) enlarges the low-data regime from 50 to 400\nkeystrokes. For each method, dashed lines indicate the\naccuracy before correction and solid lines indicate the accuracy after correction; the lightly shaded region represents the improvement obtained by feedback learning or\nLLM correction. The proposed method reaches 99.00%\naccuracy with only 100 observed keystrokes, correspond-\n\nEvaluation metrics. We evaluate reconstruction performance using the Levenshtein score, defined as a normalized complement of the edit distance (Levenshtein distance), where higher values indicate better reconstruction.\n7\n\n\f100\nLevenshtein score [%]\n\nTarget PC\n\nSmartphone\n\n80\n60\nDell Latitude 7320\nApple MacBook Pro (2019)\nHP OmniBook X14\nLenovo ThinkPad X390\n\n40\n20\n0 50\n\n100\n\n150\n\n200\n250\n300\nNumber of keystrokes\n\n350\n\n400\n\nFigure 7: Experimental results on four laptops recorded by\na smartphone.\n\n100\n\n100\n\n80\n\n80\n\n60\n40\nThis work\nHMM-based\nDictionary-based\n\n20\n0 0\n\n500\n\n1000 1500 2000\nNumber of keystrokes\n\n2500\n\n(a) Full range (50–2,400 keys).\n\nLevenshtein score [%]\n\nLevenshtein score [%]\n\nFigure 5: Experimental setup using a 13-inch MacBook\nPro (2019) and a smartphone.\n\npendix C (426, 426, and 438 characters, respectively), and\nwe recorded the keystroke sounds using the same setup as\nin Section 4.1.\nFor each recording, we ran reconstruction using only the\nfirst n keystrokes, where n ranges from 50 to 400 in increments of 50. We compute the Levenshtein score for each\nrun. Figure 7 reports the mean score across the three texts.\nOverall, our method achieves consistently high reconstruction accuracy across all four platforms once at least 150\nkeystrokes are available. This result confirms the robust\nperformance of the proposed eavesdropping attack.\n\n60\n40\n20\n0\n\n100\n\n200\n300\nNumber of keystrokes\n\n(b) Low-data\nkeys).\n\nregime\n\n400\n\n(50–400\n\nFigure 6: Evaluation results on the MacBook Pro.\n\n5. Validation in Real-World Scenarios\ning to 17 words. At this point, the pre-feedback accuracy\nis 47.00%, so feedback learning improves the score by\n52.00 percentage points. With 150 observed keystrokes,\ncorresponding to 23 words, the proposed method reaches\n99.33% accuracy, while the pre-feedback accuracy is\n62.67%, yielding an additional gain of 36.66 percentage\npoints.\n\nIn this section, we examine our attack in the three realworld scenarios proposed in Section 2: distant recording\non the same desk (Section 5.1), through-wall recording\n(Section 5.2), and online meetings (Section 5.3). We use\nthe same four laptop platforms and the same three texts as\nin Section 4.3.\n\nThe HMM-based method improves much more slowly\nand reaches 97.27% only at about 1,200 observed\nkeystrokes, corresponding to approximately 178 words.\nBeyond that point, its accuracy remains roughly unchanged. The dictionary-based method reaches 97.13% at\n350 observed keystrokes and thereafter fluctuates slightly\nwhile remaining mostly above 90%. In the text used in this\nexperiment, 350 characters correspond to approximately\n53 words, which is close to the 50-word setting reported\nby Yang et al. [38]. Although Yang et al. studied a WiFibased attack, this result indicates that a similar dictionarybased decoding effect can also be reproduced in the acoustic setting as well. Overall, the proposed method achieves\nhigh-accuracy reconstruction with substantially fewer observed characters and words than both the HMM-based\nand dictionary-based baselines.\n\nThe contact microphone used in the experiments in Section 5.1 and Section 5.2 captures structure-borne vibrations from a rigid object (here, the desk surface or wall)\nrather than airborne sound. Because vibrations can propagate over long distances within the same rigid body,\nkeystroke information can remain more observable even\nif the contact microphone is placed farther from the target, when compared with an ordinary voice recorder. This\nsensing modality also has two practical advantages for an\nattacker: (i) it is less affected by ambient acoustic noise\ncompared than voice recorders, and (ii) the contact microphone is small and easy to conceal in public and shared\nspaces (e.g., libraries and meeting rooms) by attaching it\nunobtrusively to a desk surface. These properties make\ncontact-microphone-based eavesdropping practical and realistic. We used an FL-1000 from Sun-Mechatronics [32]\nin our experiments.\n\n4.3 Evaluation on Multiple Laptop Platforms\n\n5.1 Distant Recording on Same Desk\n\nWe then evaluate the robustness of our attack to differences in keyboard and chassis design by conducting experiments on four off-the-shelf laptops: a Dell Latitude\n7320, a 13-inch MacBook Pro (2019), an HP OmniBook\nX 14, and a Lenovo ThinkPad X390. For each laptop, the\nvictim typed three different English passages shown in Ap-\n\nFigure 8(a) shows an overview of the experimental setup,\nin which the attacker is 3.0 m away from the victim on the\nsame desk surface. Figure 8(b) shows the attacker’s equipment: comprising a contact microphone, an audio amplifier, and a laptop used to record the signal. Although the\nattacker is depicted using a laptop for recording in Fig8\n\n\fVictim’s PC\n\nWall (plaster)\n\nAttacker’s PC\n\nVictim’s room\n\nContact microphone\n\nAttacker’s PC\n\nContact microphone\n\n3.0 m\n\nTarget PC\n\nContact microphone\n\n(a) Experimental setup.\n\nPC for recording\n\nAudio amplifier\n\n(b) Attacker’s equipment.\n\nFigure 8: Overview of distant keystroke recording on desk.\n\n(a) Attack concept.\n\n100\nLevenshtein score [%]\n\nAttacker’s room\n\nAttacker’s PC\n\nContact mic\n\n80\n60\nDell Latitude 7320\nApple MacBook Pro (2019)\nHP OmniBook X14\nLenovo ThinkPad X390\n\n40\n20\n0 50\n\n100\n\n150\n\n200\n250\n300\nNumber of keystrokes\n\n350\n\nAmplifier\n", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 500, "totalLines": 1980}}}
