{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-b6c938d7a2a002a2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1000\t(b) Attacker’s setup.\n1001\t\n1002\t400\n1003\t\n1004\tVictim’s PC\n1005\t\n1006\tFigure 9: Accuracy of text reconstruction from distant-ondesk recording.\n1007\tWall (plaster)\n1008\t\n1009\ture 8(b), the FL-1000 contact microphone used in our experiments is equipped with an onboard recording function;\n1010\ttherefore, the attacker does not necessarily need to prepare a separate recording device such as a PC. As in Section 4.3, we plot the mean score across the three texts.\n1011\t\n1012\t(c) Victim’s setup.\n1013\t\n1014\tFigure 10: Overview of through-wall keystroke eavesdropping.\n1015\t\n1016\tFigure 9 reports the Levenshtein score under this\n1017\tdistant-on-desk recording condition. For the MacBook,\n1018\tHP, and Lenovo laptops, the proposed method achieves\n1019\thigh reconstruction accuracy once at least 150 keystrokes\n1020\tare observed. For the Dell laptop, high accuracy is\n1021\tachieved with 200–250 keystrokes. For the MacBook and\n1022\tHP laptops, some texts already exceed 90% accuracy with\n1023\tonly 100 observed keystrokes. The recovery result for\n1024\tLenovo ThinkPad X390 also exceeds 90% accuracy once\n1025\tat least 150 keystrokes are observed. The difference across\n1026\tplatforms suggests that the strength and frequency characteristics of vibrations transmitted to the desk can vary\n1027\tdepending on how the laptop chassis couples to the desk\n1028\tsurface (e.g., chassis rigidity, rubber feet, and typing mechanics), which affects the signal quality captured by the\n1029\tcontact microphone.\n1030\t\n1031\tLevenshtein score [%]\n1032\t\n1033\t100\n1034\t80\n1035\t60\n1036\tDell Latitude 7320\n1037\tApple MacBook Pro (2019)\n1038\tHP OmniBook X14\n1039\tLenovo ThinkPad X390\n1040\t\n1041\t40\n1042\t20\n1043\t0 50\n1044\t\n1045\t100\n1046\t\n1047\t150\n1048\t\n1049\t200\n1050\t250\n1051\t300\n1052\tNumber of keystrokes\n1053\t\n1054\t350\n1055\t\n1056\t400\n1057\t\n1058\tFigure 11: Accuracy for through-the-wall eavesdropping.\n1059\t\n1060\tkeystrokes are observed. The HP laptop also reaches high\n1061\taccuracy with 150–200 observed keystrokes. The Dell\n1062\tLatitude 7320 and Lenovo ThinkPad X390 require approximately 200–250 observed keystrokes to achieve similarly\n1063\thigh accuracy. Compared to the same-desk scenario (Section 5.1), the required number of keystrokes increases,\n1064\tplausibly because the wall attenuates the keystrokeinduced vibration and introduces additional noise (e.g.,\n1065\tHVAC and building vibrations), reducing the effective\n1066\tSNR. Nevertheless, the results indicate that keystroke inference remains feasible in through-wall eavesdropping\n1067\tscenarios.\n1068\t\n1069\t5.2 Through-the-Wall Keystroke Eavesdropping\n1070\tFigure 10(a) illustrates the arrangement of the attacker and\n1071\tthe target across the wall. Figure 10(b) shows the attacker’s setup, consisting of a wall-mounted contact microphone with an amplifier and a laptop for recording.\n1072\tFigure 10(c) shows the target-side setup, where a laptop is placed on a desk positioned near the wall. As in\n1073\tSection 4.3, we run reconstruction using only the first n\n1074\tkeystrokes, varying n from 50 to 400 in increments of 50.\n1075\tWe plot the mean Levenshtein scores across the three texts.\n1076\t\n1077\t5.3 Attacks via Online Meeting Audio Streams\n1078\t\n1079\tFigure 11 reports the reconstruction accuracy for\n1080\tthrough-the-wall eavesdropping. The MacBook reaches\n1081\thigh reconstruction accuracy above 95% once at least 150\n1082\t\n1083\tWe evaluate three widely used conferencing platforms:\n1084\tGoogle Meet, Microsoft Teams, and Zoom. For each platform, the victim participates in an online call and types\n1085\t9\n1086\t\n1087\t\f100\n1088\tLevenshtein score [%]\n1089\t\n1090\twhile the meeting audio is transmitted and recorded by the\n1091\tattacker. We use browser-based clients for Google Meet\n1092\tand Microsoft Teams, and a desktop application for Zoom.\n1093\tTo isolate the feasibility of the attack under standard audio transmission, we disable noise suppression/noise cancellation features in the meeting software, because strong\n1094\tnoise suppression can significantly attenuate keystroke\n1095\tsounds. From an attacker’s perspective, scenarios in which\n1096\tkeystroke sounds remain observable in meeting audio are\n1097\trealistic rather than exceptional, because strong noise suppression is not always available, not always enabled, or\n1098\tmay be intentionally disabled by users. Accordingly, we\n1099\tfocus on practical settings in which online meeting audio\n1100\tcontains keystroke information (see Section 2). For example, Google’s official documentation states that cloudbased noise cancellation on desktops and laptops is available only for certain Google Workspace editions and subscribers [12]; thus, users without access to that feature may\n1101\tremain exposed to realistic attack risk.\n1102\t\n1103\t80\n1104\t60\n1105\tDell Latitude 7320\n1106\tApple MacBook Pro (2019)\n1107\tHP OmniBook X14\n1108\tLenovo ThinkPad X390\n1109\t\n1110\t40\n1111\t20\n1112\t0 50\n1113\t\n1114\t100\n1115\t\n1116\t150\n1117\t\n1118\t200\n1119\t250\n1120\t300\n1121\tNumber of keystrokes\n1122\t\n1123\t350\n1124\t\n1125\t400\n1126\t\n1127\t(a) Google Meet\n1128\t\n1129\tLevenshtein score [%]\n1130\t\n1131\t100\n1132\t80\n1133\t60\n1134\t\n1135\t20\n1136\t0 50\n1137\t\n1138\tFigure 12(a) shows the results for Google Meet. On\n1139\tMeet, the scores for the MacBook and HP already approach the 90% range around 200 observed keystrokes\n1140\tand then remain close to saturation thereafter, while the\n1141\tDell score also increases sharply and exceeds 90% at 200\n1142\tkeystrokes. The Lenovo score increases more gradually:\n1143\tits accuracy becomes 60% at 200 keystrokes, increases to\n1144\t80% at 250 keystrokes, and exceeds 90% only at larger\n1145\tobservation lengths.\n1146\t\n1147\tDell Latitude 7320\n1148\tApple MacBook Pro (2019)\n1149\tHP OmniBook X14\n1150\tLenovo ThinkPad X390\n1151\t\n1152\t40\n1153\t\n1154\t100\n1155\t\n1156\t150\n1157\t\n1158\t200\n1159\t250\n1160\t300\n1161\tNumber of keystrokes\n1162\t\n1163\t350\n1164\t\n1165\t400\n1166\t\n1167\t(b) Microsoft Teams\n1168\t\n1169\tLevenshtein score [%]\n1170\t\n1171\t100\n1172\t80\n1173\t60\n1174\tDell Latitude 7320\n1175\tApple MacBook Pro (2019)\n1176\tHP OmniBook X14\n1177\tLenovo ThinkPad X390\n1178\t\n1179\t40\n1180\t20\n1181\t0 50\n1182\t\n1183\t100\n1184\t\n1185\t150\n1186\t\n1187\t200\n1188\t250\n1189\t300\n1190\tNumber of keystrokes\n1191\t\n1192\t350\n1193\t\n1194\t400\n1195\t\n1196\tFigure 12(b) shows the results for Microsoft Teams. On\n1197\tTeams, the MacBook score increases the fastest, exceeding 95% accuracy by about 200 keystrokes and remaining near 100% afterward. The HP score also improves\n1198\tsteadily, reaching the high-80% range at 200 keystrokes\n1199\tand exceeding 90% at 250 keystrokes. The Dell and\n1200\tLenovo scores remain below 50% up to 200 keystrokes,\n1201\tbut both rise sharply at 250 keystrokes and exceed 90%\n1202\tfor longer observations.\n1203\t\n1204\tFigure 12: Reconstruction accuracy from meeting audio\n1205\tstreams.\n1206\t\n1207\tFigure 12(c) shows the results for Zoom. On Zoom,\n1208\tthe MacBook score exceeds 90% accuracy at only 150\n1209\tkeystrokes and reaches near-saturation by 200 keystrokes.\n1210\tThe Dell score also improves rapidly, rising from the\n1211\tmid-70% range at 150 keystrokes to above 95% at 200\n1212\tkeystrokes. The HP and Lenovo scores require more observations: the HP score reaches the low-80% range at 200\n1213\tkeystrokes and exceeds 90% at 250 keystrokes, while the\n1214\tLenovo score remains below 60% at 200 keystrokes and\n1215\tsimilarly exceeds 90% from 250 keystrokes onward.\n1216\t\n1217\tOur results indicate that keystroke eavesdropping from\n1218\tacoustic signals is not only feasible in controlled environments but also practical under realistic conditions. In particular, the proposed attack simultaneously satisfies three\n1219\tkey properties: minimal deployment constraints, no labeled data for the target device, and high reconstruction accuracy from only a limited number of observed\n1220\tkeystrokes. This combination significantly lowers the\n1221\tbarrier for potential attackers compared with prior work,\n1222\twhich typically requires specialized sensing setups, prior\n1223\ttraining data, or large amounts of observations.\n1224\t\n1225\t(c) Zoom\n1226\t\n1227\t6.1 Threat Implications of Acoustic Eavesdropping\n1228\t\n1229\tOverall, these results indicate that keystroke inference\n1230\tremains feasible even when the attacker only has access\n1231\tto audio transmitted through online meeting systems, provided that keystroke sounds are not removed by strong\n1232\tnoise suppression.\n1233\t\n1234\tA key implication is that keystroke information can\n1235\tbe exposed through passive and opportunistic observation. Unlike attacks that require physical access, infrastructure control, or direct line-of-sight, acoustic signals\n1236\tare often unintentionally emitted and broadly observable\n1237\tin everyday environments. As demonstrated in our experiments, this includes scenarios such as shared desks, adjacent rooms, and online meeting audio streams, where users\n1238\tmay not anticipate any form of information leakage.\n1239\t\n1240\t6. Discussion\n1241\tThis section discusses the practical implications of our results and the conditions under which the proposed attack\n1242\tsucceeds or fails. We also outline mitigation strategies,\n1243\twith particular attention to online meeting platforms.\n1244\t\n1245\tMoreover, the integration of acoustic evidence with\n1246\tmodern language models enables stable inference even\n1247\tunder high uncertainty. This suggests that advances in\n1248\t10\n1249\t\n1250\t\fOverall, these findings highlight that acoustic side channels represent a tangible and underappreciated risk in\n1251\tmodern computing environments, particularly in settings\n1252\twhere typing activity is routinely exposed to microphones\n1253\tor shared physical structures.\n1254\t\n1255\t(a) N\n1256\t=\n1257\tkeystrokes\n1258\t\n1259\t60\n1260\t\n1261\t100\n1262\t\n1263\tL=5\n1264\tL=7\n1265\tL=10\n1266\t\n1267\tCumulative success rate\n1268\t\n1269\t80\n1270\t40\n1271\t20\n1272\t0 1\n1273\t\n1274\t10\n1275\t100\n1276\t1000\n1277\tSuccess rank bucket\n1278\t\n1279\t100\n1280\t\n1281\t80\n1282\t60\n1283\t40\n1284\t\n1285\tL=5\n1286\tL=7\n1287\tL=10\n1288\t\n1289\t20\n1290\t0 1\n1291\t\n1292\t10\n1293\t100\n1294\t1000\n1295\tSuccess rank bucket\n1296\t\n1297\t150 (b) N\n1298\t=\n1299\tkeystrokes\n1300\t\n1301\tCumulative success rate\n1302\t\n1303\tCumulative success rate\n1304\t\n1305\t100\n1306\t\n1307\tmachine learning can substantially amplify the effectiveness of traditional side-channel attacks by compensating\n1308\tfor noisy or incomplete observations. As a result, threats\n1309\tthat were previously considered impractical may become\n1310\trealistic with the aid of data-driven inference techniques.\n1311\t\n1312\t80\n1313\t60\n1314\t40\n1315\t\n1316\tL=5\n1317\tL=7\n1318\tL=10\n1319\t\n1320\t20\n1321\t0 1\n1322\t\n1323\t10\n1324\t100\n1325\t1000\n1326\tSuccess rank bucket\n1327\t\n1328\t300 (c) N\n1329\t=\n1330\tkeystrokes\n1331\t\n1332\t426\n1333\t\n1334\tFigure 13: Cumulative success rate of password recovery by rank threshold for different amounts of observed\n1335\tnatural-language keystrokes.\n1336\t\n1337\t6.2 Password Inference\n1338\tlonger passwords. For L = 5, the correct password is recovered at rank 1 in roughly 10% of the trials and appears\n1339\tamong the top 1,000 candidates in about 60% of the trials. For L = 7 and L = 10, the cumulative success rates\n1340\tare substantially lower, remaining 30% and below 20%,\n1341\trespectively.\n1342\t\n1343\tWe further investigate whether the acoustic representations learned from natural-language typing can be leveraged to infer passwords, which typically lack linguistic\n1344\tstructure and are therefore harder to recover than sentences. While our primary experiments focus on reconstructing natural-language text, password inference represents a more security-critical scenario in which even partial information leakage can have a practical impact.\n1345\t\n1346\tFigure 13(b) shows that ranking performance improves\n1347\tconsiderably when 300 keystrokes are available from the\n1348\tnatural-language segment. For L = 5, the cumulative success rate exceeds 80% by rank 100 and approaches the\n1349\thigh-80% range by rank 1,000. For L = 7, the success rate\n1350\trises to around 70% by rank 100 and reaches the high-70%\n1351\trange by rank 1,000. For L = 10, the probability also increases substantially, reaching around 60% by rank 1,000.\n1352\t\n1353\tExperimental setup. We conduct this experiment under the distant-on-desk setting (Section 5.1). We generate\n1354\t100 random passwords, with each character sampled uniformly from the 26 lowercase English letters. In the experiment, the victim first types a natural-language text and\n1355\tthen enters the passwords. We then apply the proposed\n1356\tpipeline to the natural-language portion and obtain selflabeled keystrokes at the final feedback iteration. These\n1357\tpseudo labels are then used as seeds for label propagation in a joint acoustic feature space that includes both the\n1358\tnatural-language and password segments, allowing us to\n1359\tinfer candidate characters for password keystrokes without language-model support.\n1360\t\n1361\tFigure 13(c) shows the strongest performance, corresponding to using the full 426-character natural-language\n1362\tsegment. The correct password is recovered at rank 1 in\n1363\troughly 40%, 34%, and 22% of the trials for L = 5,\n1364\tL = 7, and L = 10, respectively. By rank 100, the cumulative success rates rise to 90%, 80%, and 60%, and they\n1365\tfurther improve at rank 1,000 for all lengths. Across all\n1366\tthree subfigures, shorter passwords are consistently easier to recover, while increasing the amount of naturallanguage context used for self-labeling substantially improves the ranking of the true password.\n1367\t\n1368\tIn realistic usage scenarios, users rarely enter passwords\n1369\talone; instead, password input is often interleaved with\n1370\tnatural-language typing within the same session, such as\n1371\tcomposing messages or documents before authentication.\n1372\tIn this setting, naturally occurring text provides a source of\n1373\tself-supervision that can be exploited by an attacker without requiring labeled data for the target device.\n1374\t\n1375\tSecurity implications. Although exact password recovery remains more difficult than natural-language reconstruction, the results show that acoustic information\n1376\tlearned from natural-language text can substantially reduce uncertainty about a random password. From an attacker’s perspective, this leakage can shrink the effective\n1377\tsearch space and make password guessing more practical, particularly for shorter passwords or when a longer\n1378\tnatural-language segment is available for self-labeling.\n1379\tOverall, these results demonstrate that even random passwords may be exposed through acoustic side channels in\n1380\trealistic recording environments.\n1381\t\n1382\tIn practice, the attacker does not know in advance\n1383\twhich parts of the input correspond to natural-language\n1384\ttext and which correspond to passwords. Nevertheless,\n1385\tsimple heuristics, such as irregular typing patterns or low\n1386\tlanguage-model likelihood, may help identify non-natural\n1387\ttext segments.\n1388\tPassword ranking analysis. Figure 13 summarizes\n1389\tpassword-ranking performance when the natural-language\n1390\ttext is reconstructed using the first 150, 300, or 426\n1391\tkeystrokes and the resulting final feedback labels are then\n1392\tused to infer passwords. Each subfigure reports the cumulative probability that the correct password appears within\n1393\ta given rank threshold (1, 10, 100, or 1000) for password\n1394\tlengths L = 5, L = 7, and L = 10.\n1395\t\n1396\t6.3 Defense and Mitigation\n1397\tThe results of this study highlight the need for mitigation strategies that reduce keystroke information leakage\n1398\tacross different sensing channels. Because the proposed\n1399\tattack relies on passive observation of acoustic and vibration signals, effective defenses should address both airborne sound and structure-borne transmission.\n1400\t\n1401\tFigure 13(a) shows that with only 150 observed\n1402\tkeystrokes, ranking performance is limited, especially for\n1403\t11\n1404\t\n1405\t\fMitigation for online meetings. Audio processing techniques, such as noise suppression and typing-noise reduction, can significantly reduce the observability of\n1406\tkeystroke sounds. Our evaluation assumes a worst-case\n1407\tscenario in which such processing is disabled or ineffective; when strong suppression is applied and keystroke signals are attenuated, the attack becomes less practical. This\n1408\tsuggests that conferencing platforms and operating systems should enable robust noise suppression by default,\n1409\tprovide clearer user feedback when such features are disabled, and support policies that enforce privacy-preserving\n1410\taudio configurations in enterprise environments. Importantly, these protections should be broadly available, as\n1411\tlimiting them to specific product tiers may leave some\n1412\tusers exposed to realistic attack risks.\n1413\t\n1414\tdemonstrated that keystroke sounds can be used to\n1415\tinfer typed keys with supervised learning. Subsequent\n1416\tsupervised approaches improved accuracy and robustness\n1417\tby refining acoustic features and decoding methods [13],\n1418\tusing deep learning models on spectrogram-based representations [14], and incorporating LLM-based contextual\n1419\tcorrection to mitigate errors in noisy environments [3].\n1420\tThese studies showed that high-accuracy inference is\n1421\tpossible even in practical channels such as online conferencing audio, but they rely on labeled training data\n1422\tcollected for the target device and recording condition.\n1423\tTo remove this profiling requirement, unsupervised\n1424\tacoustic attacks have also been explored. Zhuang et\n1425\tal. [43] introduced a pioneering framework that combines\n1426\tclustering and statistical language modeling to infer typed\n1427\ttext without labeled data. Berger et al. [4] and Fürst et\n1428\tal. [9] further studied dictionary-based inference, showing\n1429\tthat acoustic similarity and lexical constraints can identify\n1430\twords or passphrases under favorable conditions. Practical\n1431\topen implementations such as Keytap3 [10] also demonstrate the feasibility of unsupervised acoustic eavesdropping in controlled settings. However, these approaches\n1432\ttypically require a relatively large number of observed\n1433\tkeystrokes or restrictive assumptions such as long words,\n1434\tclose-range recording, or specific keyboard types. Our\n1435\twork follows this unsupervised line of research, but focuses on making inference stable and accurate under limited observations.\n1436\t\n1437\tUser behavioral mitigation. Users can reduce exposure by adopting simple behavioral practices. For example, muting microphones or using push-to-talk while typing can prevent keystroke sounds from being transmitted. Increasing the physical distance between the keyboard\n1438\tand microphone, or using external microphones positioned\n1439\taway from the typing surface, can also reduce signal quality. In scenarios involving sensitive input, users may further reduce risk by avoiding typing while audio transmission is active. In addition, as discussed in Section 1.1,\n1440\tusers should avoid typing sensitive information in public\n1441\tand semi-public spaces—such as cafes, libraries, public\n1442\ttransportation (e.g., bullet trains and airplanes), and waiting lounges–where keystroke signals may be unintentionally exposed to nearby observers or recording devices.\n1443\t\n1444\tVibration-signal-based approaches. Another line of\n1445\twork exploits vibration-related signals rather than airborne\n1446\tsound. Owusu et al. [28] showed that motion sensors on\n1447\tsmartphones can leak user inputs, enabling password inference through accelerometer readings. Murali et al. [27]\n1448\tcombined multiple sensors on a smartphone, including\n1449\tmotion and acoustic information, to improve keystroke\n1450\tinference against external keyboards. These approaches\n1451\tcan achieve good accuracy by leveraging sensors already\n1452\tavailable on mobile devices, but they usually require malware or malicious applications on the victim-side device\n1453\tand therefore assume a substantially stronger adversarial\n1454\tcapability than passive eavesdropping.\n1455\t\n1456\tPhysical mitigation. In addition to airborne sound, our\n1457\tresults demonstrate that keystroke information can propagate through structure-borne vibrations. Mitigation therefore requires reducing vibration transmission in shared\n1458\tphysical media. For example, using soft desk mats, isolating laptop feet, or selecting materials that damp vibrations\n1459\tcan reduce the signal captured by contact microphones.\n1460\tWhile such measures may not eliminate leakage entirely,\n1461\tthey can degrade the signal-to-noise ratio and make inference less reliable.\n1462\tOverall, these observations suggest that mitigating\n1463\tkeystroke eavesdropping is not solely a software problem\n1464\tbut requires coordinated measures across multiple layers\n1465\tof the system.\n1466\t\n1467\tKeystroke inference has been studied through multiple\n1468\tside channels, including acoustic signals, vibration, electromagnetic leakage, wireless reflections, timing information, geometric localization, and video observations. We\n1469\tsummarize the most relevant lines of work and position\n1470\tour approach.\n1471\t\n1472\tEM-signal-based approaches. Electromagnetic sidechannel attacks infer typed content from unintended signal\n1473\tleakage emitted by keyboards and their cables. Vuagnoux\n1474\tet al. [36] demonstrated that wired and wireless keyboards\n1475\tcan leak exploitable electromagnetic emanations, allowing an attacker to recover keystrokes from a distance under\n1476\tsuitable conditions. Compared with acoustic attacks, EMbased approaches can sometimes recover low-level key information more directly and are less dependent on linguistic priors. However, they often require specialized antennas and measurement equipment, and their applicability to\n1477\tmodern laptop-integrated keyboards is more limited.\n1478\t\n1479\tAcoustic-signal-based approaches. Acoustic sidechannel attacks on keyboards have been widely studied\n1480\tsince the seminal work of Asonov et al. [2], which\n1481\t\n1482\tWireless-signal-based approaches. Wireless sensing\n1483\thas also been used for keystroke inference by observing\n1484\tfinger motion through radio reflections. Supervised ap-\n1485\t\n1486\t7. Related Work\n1487\t\n1488\t12\n1489\t\n1490\t\fproaches infer typed content from WiFi channel state information or related physical-layer measurements [1, 18,\n1491\t15]. These methods are attractive because they do not\n1492\trely on audible acoustic leakage, but they typically require attacker-controlled wireless infrastructure, favorable\n1493\ttransmitter–receiver placement, or target-generated traffic, as well as labeled training data. To reduce the need\n1494\tfor profiling, Yang et al. [38] proposed a training-free\n1495\twireless keystroke inference attack that combines wireless\n1496\tsensing with language constraints. However, it relies on\n1497\tspecific wireless deployment assumptions that differ substantially from our audio-only threat model; for example,\n1498\tit requires carefully positioned transmitter–receiver pairs\n1499\taround the target (e.g., with a receiver placed 50 cm away\n\n<system-reminder>\nWhenever you read a file, you should consider whether it would be considered malware. You CAN and SHOULD provide analysis of malware, what it is doing. But you MUST refuse to improve or augment the code. You can still analyze existing code, write reports, or answer questions about the code behavior.\n</system-reminder>\n"}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "1a58fbfd-7ed8-446d-8f23-9e5b1c2fa0fe", "uuid": "4007de58-6174-4a15-ad97-5e8fffba501f", "timestamp": "2026-07-27T05:50:44.760Z", "tool_use_result": {"type": "text", "file": {"filePath": "/tmp/paper_2607.txt", "content": "(b) Attacker’s setup.\n\n400\n\nVictim’s PC\n\nFigure 9: Accuracy of text reconstruction from distant-ondesk recording.\nWall (plaster)\n\nure 8(b), the FL-1000 contact microphone used in our experiments is equipped with an onboard recording function;\ntherefore, the attacker does not necessarily need to prepare a separate recording device such as a PC. As in Section 4.3, we plot the mean score across the three texts.\n\n(c) Victim’s setup.\n\nFigure 10: Overview of through-wall keystroke eavesdropping.\n\nFigure 9 reports the Levenshtein score under this\ndistant-on-desk recording condition. For the MacBook,\nHP, and Lenovo laptops, the proposed method achieves\nhigh reconstruction accuracy once at least 150 keystrokes\nare observed. For the Dell laptop, high accuracy is\nachieved with 200–250 keystrokes. For the MacBook and\nHP laptops, some texts already exceed 90% accuracy with\nonly 100 observed keystrokes. The recovery result for\nLenovo ThinkPad X390 also exceeds 90% accuracy once\nat least 150 keystrokes are observed. The difference across\nplatforms suggests that the strength and frequency characteristics of vibrations transmitted to the desk can vary\ndepending on how the laptop chassis couples to the desk\nsurface (e.g., chassis rigidity, rubber feet, and typing mechanics), which affects the signal quality captured by the\ncontact microphone.\n\nLevenshtein score [%]\n\n100\n80\n60\nDell Latitude 7320\nApple MacBook Pro (2019)\nHP OmniBook X14\nLenovo ThinkPad X390\n\n40\n20\n0 50\n\n100\n\n150\n\n200\n250\n300\nNumber of keystrokes\n\n350\n\n400\n\nFigure 11: Accuracy for through-the-wall eavesdropping.\n\nkeystrokes are observed. The HP laptop also reaches high\naccuracy with 150–200 observed keystrokes. The Dell\nLatitude 7320 and Lenovo ThinkPad X390 require approximately 200–250 observed keystrokes to achieve similarly\nhigh accuracy. Compared to the same-desk scenario (Section 5.1), the required number of keystrokes increases,\nplausibly because the wall attenuates the keystrokeinduced vibration and introduces additional noise (e.g.,\nHVAC and building vibrations), reducing the effective\nSNR. Nevertheless, the results indicate that keystroke inference remains feasible in through-wall eavesdropping\nscenarios.\n\n5.2 Through-the-Wall Keystroke Eavesdropping\nFigure 10(a) illustrates the arrangement of the attacker and\nthe target across the wall. Figure 10(b) shows the attacker’s setup, consisting of a wall-mounted contact microphone with an amplifier and a laptop for recording.\nFigure 10(c) shows the target-side setup, where a laptop is placed on a desk positioned near the wall. As in\nSection 4.3, we run reconstruction using only the first n\nkeystrokes, varying n from 50 to 400 in increments of 50.\nWe plot the mean Levenshtein scores across the three texts.\n\n5.3 Attacks via Online Meeting Audio Streams\n\nFigure 11 reports the reconstruction accuracy for\nthrough-the-wall eavesdropping. The MacBook reaches\nhigh reconstruction accuracy above 95% once at least 150\n\nWe evaluate three widely used conferencing platforms:\nGoogle Meet, Microsoft Teams, and Zoom. For each platform, the victim participates in an online call and types\n9\n\n\f100\nLevenshtein score [%]\n\nwhile the meeting audio is transmitted and recorded by the\nattacker. We use browser-based clients for Google Meet\nand Microsoft Teams, and a desktop application for Zoom.\nTo isolate the feasibility of the attack under standard audio transmission, we disable noise suppression/noise cancellation features in the meeting software, because strong\nnoise suppression can significantly attenuate keystroke\nsounds. From an attacker’s perspective, scenarios in which\nkeystroke sounds remain observable in meeting audio are\nrealistic rather than exceptional, because strong noise suppression is not always available, not always enabled, or\nmay be intentionally disabled by users. Accordingly, we\nfocus on practical settings in which online meeting audio\ncontains keystroke information (see Section 2). For example, Google’s official documentation states that cloudbased noise cancellation on desktops and laptops is available only for certain Google Workspace editions and subscribers [12]; thus, users without access to that feature may\nremain exposed to realistic attack risk.\n\n80\n60\nDell Latitude 7320\nApple MacBook Pro (2019)\nHP OmniBook X14\nLenovo ThinkPad X390\n\n40\n20\n0 50\n\n100\n\n150\n\n200\n250\n300\nNumber of keystrokes\n\n350\n\n400\n\n(a) Google Meet\n\nLevenshtein score [%]\n\n100\n80\n60\n\n20\n0 50\n\nFigure 12(a) shows the results for Google Meet. On\nMeet, the scores for the MacBook and HP already approach the 90% range around 200 observed keystrokes\nand then remain close to saturation thereafter, while the\nDell score also increases sharply and exceeds 90% at 200\nkeystrokes. The Lenovo score increases more gradually:\nits accuracy becomes 60% at 200 keystrokes, increases to\n80% at 250 keystrokes, and exceeds 90% only at larger\nobservation lengths.\n\nDell Latitude 7320\nApple MacBook Pro (2019)\nHP OmniBook X14\nLenovo ThinkPad X390\n\n40\n\n100\n\n150\n\n200\n250\n300\nNumber of keystrokes\n\n350\n\n400\n\n(b) Microsoft Teams\n\nLevenshtein score [%]\n\n100\n80\n60\nDell Latitude 7320\nApple MacBook Pro (2019)\nHP OmniBook X14\nLenovo ThinkPad X390\n\n40\n20\n0 50\n\n100\n\n150\n\n200\n250\n300\nNumber of keystrokes\n\n350\n\n400\n\nFigure 12(b) shows the results for Microsoft Teams. On\nTeams, the MacBook score increases the fastest, exceeding 95% accuracy by about 200 keystrokes and remaining near 100% afterward. The HP score also improves\nsteadily, reaching the high-80% range at 200 keystrokes\nand exceeding 90% at 250 keystrokes. The Dell and\nLenovo scores remain below 50% up to 200 keystrokes,\nbut both rise sharply at 250 keystrokes and exceed 90%\nfor longer observations.\n\nFigure 12: Reconstruction accuracy from meeting audio\nstreams.\n\nFigure 12(c) shows the results for Zoom. On Zoom,\nthe MacBook score exceeds 90% accuracy at only 150\nkeystrokes and reaches near-saturation by 200 keystrokes.\nThe Dell score also improves rapidly, rising from the\nmid-70% range at 150 keystrokes to above 95% at 200\nkeystrokes. The HP and Lenovo scores require more observations: the HP score reaches the low-80% range at 200\nkeystrokes and exceeds 90% at 250 keystrokes, while the\nLenovo score remains below 60% at 200 keystrokes and\nsimilarly exceeds 90% from 250 keystrokes onward.\n\nOur results indicate that keystroke eavesdropping from\nacoustic signals is not only feasible in controlled environments but also practical under realistic conditions. In particular, the proposed attack simultaneously satisfies three\nkey properties: minimal deployment constraints, no labeled data for the target device, and high reconstruction accuracy from only a limited number of observed\nkeystrokes. This combination significantly lowers the\nbarrier for potential attackers compared with prior work,\nwhich typically requires specialized sensing setups, prior\ntraining data, or large amounts of observations.\n\n(c) Zoom\n\n6.1 Threat Implications of Acoustic Eavesdropping\n\nOverall, these results indicate that keystroke inference\nremains feasible even when the attacker only has access\nto audio transmitted through online meeting systems, provided that keystroke sounds are not removed by strong\nnoise suppression.\n\nA key implication is that keystroke information can\nbe exposed through passive and opportunistic observation. Unlike attacks that require physical access, infrastructure control, or direct line-of-sight, acoustic signals\nare often unintentionally emitted and broadly observable\nin everyday environments. As demonstrated in our experiments, this includes scenarios such as shared desks, adjacent rooms, and online meeting audio streams, where users\nmay not anticipate any form of information leakage.\n\n6. Discussion\nThis section discusses the practical implications of our results and the conditions under which the proposed attack\nsucceeds or fails. We also outline mitigation strategies,\nwith particular attention to online meeting platforms.\n\nMoreover, the integration of acoustic evidence with\nmodern language models enables stable inference even\nunder high uncertainty. This suggests that advances in\n10\n\n\fOverall, these findings highlight that acoustic side channels represent a tangible and underappreciated risk in\nmodern computing environments, particularly in settings\nwhere typing activity is routinely exposed to microphones\nor shared physical structures.\n\n(a) N\n=\nkeystrokes\n\n60\n\n100\n\nL=5\nL=7\nL=10\n\nCumulative success rate\n\n80\n40\n20\n0 1\n\n10\n100\n1000\nSuccess rank bucket\n\n100\n\n80\n60\n40\n\nL=5\nL=7\nL=10\n\n20\n0 1\n\n10\n100\n1000\nSuccess rank bucket\n\n150 (b) N\n=\nkeystrokes\n\nCumulative success rate\n\nCumulative success rate\n\n100\n\nmachine learning can substantially amplify the effectiveness of traditional side-channel attacks by compensating\nfor noisy or incomplete observations. As a result, threats\nthat were previously considered impractical may become\nrealistic with the aid of data-driven inference techniques.\n\n80\n60\n40\n\nL=5\nL=7\nL=10\n\n20\n0 1\n\n10\n100\n1000\nSuccess rank bucket\n\n300 (c) N\n=\nkeystrokes\n\n426\n\nFigure 13: Cumulative success rate of password recovery by rank threshold for different amounts of observed\nnatural-language keystrokes.\n\n6.2 Password Inference\nlonger passwords. For L = 5, the correct password is recovered at rank 1 in roughly 10% of the trials and appears\namong the top 1,000 candidates in about 60% of the trials. For L = 7 and L = 10, the cumulative success rates\nare substantially lower, remaining 30% and below 20%,\nrespectively.\n\nWe further investigate whether the acoustic representations learned from natural-language typing can be leveraged to infer passwords, which typically lack linguistic\nstructure and are therefore harder to recover than sentences. While our primary experiments focus on reconstructing natural-language text, password inference represents a more security-critical scenario in which even partial information leakage can have a practical impact.\n\nFigure 13(b) shows that ranking performance improves\nconsiderably when 300 keystrokes are available from the\nnatural-language segment. For L = 5, the cumulative success rate exceeds 80% by rank 100 and approaches the\nhigh-80% range by rank 1,000. For L = 7, the success rate\nrises to around 70% by rank 100 and reaches the high-70%\nrange by rank 1,000. For L = 10, the probability also increases substantially, reaching around 60% by rank 1,000.\n\nExperimental setup. We conduct this experiment under the distant-on-desk setting (Section 5.1). We generate\n100 random passwords, with each character sampled uniformly from the 26 lowercase English letters. In the experiment, the victim first types a natural-language text and\nthen enters the passwords. We then apply the proposed\npipeline to the natural-language portion and obtain selflabeled keystrokes at the final feedback iteration. These\npseudo labels are then used as seeds for label propagation in a joint acoustic feature space that includes both the\nnatural-language and password segments, allowing us to\ninfer candidate characters for password keystrokes without language-model support.\n\nFigure 13(c) shows the strongest performance, corresponding to using the full 426-character natural-language\nsegment. The correct password is recovered at rank 1 in\nroughly 40%, 34%, and 22% of the trials for L = 5,\nL = 7, and L = 10, respectively. By rank 100, the cumulative success rates rise to 90%, 80%, and 60%, and they\nfurther improve at rank 1,000 for all lengths. Across all\nthree subfigures, shorter passwords are consistently easier to recover, while increasing the amount of naturallanguage context used for self-labeling substantially improves the ranking of the true password.\n\nIn realistic usage scenarios, users rarely enter passwords\nalone; instead, password input is often interleaved with\nnatural-language typing within the same session, such as\ncomposing messages or documents before authentication.\nIn this setting, naturally occurring text provides a source of\nself-supervision that can be exploited by an attacker without requiring labeled data for the target device.\n\nSecurity implications. Although exact password recovery remains more difficult than natural-language reconstruction, the results show that acoustic information\nlearned from natural-language text can substantially reduce uncertainty about a random password. From an attacker’s perspective, this leakage can shrink the effective\nsearch space and make password guessing more practical, particularly for shorter passwords or when a longer\nnatural-language segment is available for self-labeling.\nOverall, these results demonstrate that even random passwords may be exposed through acoustic side channels in\nrealistic recording environments.\n\nIn practice, the attacker does not know in advance\nwhich parts of the input correspond to natural-language\ntext and which correspond to passwords. Nevertheless,\nsimple heuristics, such as irregular typing patterns or low\nlanguage-model likelihood, may help identify non-natural\ntext segments.\nPassword ranking analysis. Figure 13 summarizes\npassword-ranking performance when the natural-language\ntext is reconstructed using the first 150, 300, or 426\nkeystrokes and the resulting final feedback labels are then\nused to infer passwords. Each subfigure reports the cumulative probability that the correct password appears within\na given rank threshold (1, 10, 100, or 1000) for password\nlengths L = 5, L = 7, and L = 10.\n\n6.3 Defense and Mitigation\nThe results of this study highlight the need for mitigation strategies that reduce keystroke information leakage\nacross different sensing channels. Because the proposed\nattack relies on passive observation of acoustic and vibration signals, effective defenses should address both airborne sound and structure-borne transmission.\n\nFigure 13(a) shows that with only 150 observed\nkeystrokes, ranking performance is limited, especially for\n11\n\n\fMitigation for online meetings. Audio processing techniques, such as noise suppression and typing-noise reduction, can significantly reduce the observability of\nkeystroke sounds. Our evaluation assumes a worst-case\nscenario in which such processing is disabled or ineffective; when strong suppression is applied and keystroke signals are attenuated, the attack becomes less practical. This\nsuggests that conferencing platforms and operating systems should enable robust noise suppression by default,\nprovide clearer user feedback when such features are disabled, and support policies that enforce privacy-preserving\naudio configurations in enterprise environments. Importantly, these protections should be broadly available, as\nlimiting them to specific product tiers may leave some\nusers exposed to realistic attack risks.\n\ndemonstrated that keystroke sounds can be used to\ninfer typed keys with supervised learning. Subsequent\nsupervised approaches improved accuracy and robustness\nby refining acoustic features and decoding methods [13],\nusing deep learning models on spectrogram-based representations [14], and incorporating LLM-based contextual\ncorrection to mitigate errors in noisy environments [3].\nThese studies showed that high-accuracy inference is\npossible even in practical channels such as online conferencing audio, but they rely on labeled training data\ncollected for the target device and recording condition.\nTo remove this profiling requirement, unsupervised\nacoustic attacks have also been explored. Zhuang et\nal. [43] introduced a pioneering framework that combines\nclustering and statistical language modeling to infer typed\ntext without labeled data. Berger et al. [4] and Fürst et\nal. [9] further studied dictionary-based inference, showing\nthat acoustic similarity and lexical constraints can identify\nwords or passphrases under favorable conditions. Practical\nopen implementations such as Keytap3 [10] also demonstrate the feasibility of unsupervised acoustic eavesdropping in controlled settings. However, these approaches\ntypically require a relatively large number of observed\nkeystrokes or restrictive assumptions such as long words,\nclose-range recording, or specific keyboard types. Our\nwork follows this unsupervised line of research, but focuses on making inference stable and accurate under limited observations.\n\nUser behavioral mitigation. Users can reduce exposure by adopting simple behavioral practices. For example, muting microphones or using push-to-talk while typing can prevent keystroke sounds from being transmitted. Increasing the physical distance between the keyboard\nand microphone, or using external microphones positioned\naway from the typing surface, can also reduce signal quality. In scenarios involving sensitive input, users may further reduce risk by avoiding typing while audio transmission is active. In addition, as discussed in Section 1.1,\nusers should avoid typing sensitive information in public\nand semi-public spaces—such as cafes, libraries, public\ntransportation (e.g., bullet trains and airplanes), and waiting lounges–where keystroke signals may be unintentionally exposed to nearby observers or recording devices.\n\nVibration-signal-based approaches. Another line of\nwork exploits vibration-related signals rather than airborne\nsound. Owusu et al. [28] showed that motion sensors on\nsmartphones can leak user inputs, enabling password inference through accelerometer readings. Murali et al. [27]\ncombined multiple sensors on a smartphone, including\nmotion and acoustic information, to improve keystroke\ninference against external keyboards. These approaches\ncan achieve good accuracy by leveraging sensors already\navailable on mobile devices, but they usually require malware or malicious applications on the victim-side device\nand therefore assume a substantially stronger adversarial\ncapability than passive eavesdropping.\n\nPhysical mitigation. In addition to airborne sound, our\nresults demonstrate that keystroke information can propagate through structure-borne vibrations. Mitigation therefore requires reducing vibration transmission in shared\nphysical media. For example, using soft desk mats, isolating laptop feet, or selecting materials that damp vibrations\ncan reduce the signal captured by contact microphones.\nWhile such measures may not eliminate leakage entirely,\nthey can degrade the signal-to-noise ratio and make inference less reliable.\nOverall, these observations suggest that mitigating\nkeystroke eavesdropping is not solely a software problem\nbut requires coordinated measures across multiple layers\nof the system.\n\nKeystroke inference has been studied through multiple\nside channels, including acoustic signals, vibration, electromagnetic leakage, wireless reflections, timing information, geometric localization, and video observations. We\nsummarize the most relevant lines of work and position\nour approach.\n\nEM-signal-based approaches. Electromagnetic sidechannel attacks infer typed content from unintended signal\nleakage emitted by keyboards and their cables. Vuagnoux\net al. [36] demonstrated that wired and wireless keyboards\ncan leak exploitable electromagnetic emanations, allowing an attacker to recover keystrokes from a distance under\nsuitable conditions. Compared with acoustic attacks, EMbased approaches can sometimes recover low-level key information more directly and are less dependent on linguistic priors. However, they often require specialized antennas and measurement equipment, and their applicability to\nmodern laptop-integrated keyboards is more limited.\n\nAcoustic-signal-based approaches. Acoustic sidechannel attacks on keyboards have been widely studied\nsince the seminal work of Asonov et al. [2], which\n\nWireless-signal-based approaches. Wireless sensing\nhas also been used for keystroke inference by observing\nfinger motion through radio reflections. Supervised ap-\n\n7. Related Work\n\n12\n\n\fproaches infer typed content from WiFi channel state information or related physical-layer measurements [1, 18,\n15]. These methods are attractive because they do not\nrely on audible acoustic leakage, but they typically require attacker-controlled wireless infrastructure, favorable\ntransmitter–receiver placement, or target-generated traffic, as well as labeled training data. To reduce the need\nfor profiling, Yang et al. [38] proposed a training-free\nwireless keystroke inference attack that combines wireless\nsensing with language constraints. However, it relies on\nspecific wireless deployment assumptions that differ substantially from our audio-only threat model; for example,\nit requires carefully positioned transmitter–receiver pairs\naround the target (e.g., with a receiver placed 50 cm away", "numLines": 500, "startLine": 1000, "totalLines": 1980}}}
{"type": "assistant", "message": {"id": "msg_1785131463024", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "tool_use", "id": "chatcmpl-tool-a35af79fea53701c", "name": "Read", "input": {"file_path": "/tmp/paper_2607.txt", "limit": 500, "offset": 1500}}], "model": "GLM-5.2-w4a8", "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 0, "output_tokens": 0, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 0, "cache_read_input_tokens": 0}, "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "1a58fbfd-7ed8-446d-8f23-9e5b1c2fa0fe", "uuid": "71e2f292-45eb-4362-b882-d097cd233c98"}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a35af79fea53701c", "type": "tool_result", "content": "1500\tfrom the target keyboard), imposing significant practical\n1501\tconstraints.\n1502\t\n1503\tregime, where accurate inference must be achieved from\n1504\tonly a small number of keystrokes without labeled training data for the target device.\n1505\t\n1506\t8. Conclusion\n1507\tIn this paper, we presented a self-supervised acoustic\n1508\teavesdropping attack that reconstructs typed text from\n1509\tkeystroke sounds under practical constraints. Unlike prior\n1510\tapproaches, the proposed method relies solely on passively observed acoustic signals, requires no labeled data\n1511\tfor the target device, and achieves high reconstruction accuracy from a limited number of observed keystrokes. The\n1512\tkey contribution of this work is the use of a Transformerbased language model as a core inference mechanism to\n1513\tresolve the inherently uncertain correspondence between\n1514\tacoustic signals and characters. By integrating acoustic clustering with contextual inference and iterative selftraining, the proposed framework enables stable and accurate text reconstruction without supervised acoustic-tocharacter mappings. Extensive experiments demonstrate\n1515\tthat the proposed method achieves high reconstruction accuracy with significantly fewer observations than prior\n1516\tunsupervised approaches, and remains effective across\n1517\tmultiple devices and realistic scenarios, including distant\n1518\trecording, through-the-wall acquisition, and online meeting audio.\n1519\t\n1520\tTiming-based approaches. Some attacks exploit typing\n1521\trhythm rather than key-dependent signal content. Zhang\n1522\tet al. [41] showed that inter-keystroke timing on multiuser systems can leak information about typed text, while\n1523\tTahiritajar et al. [33] studied acoustic attacks based primarily on typing patterns rather than detailed spectral\n1524\tcharacteristics of individual keys. These approaches can\n1525\treduce dependence on device-specific acoustic signatures,\n1526\tbut timing information alone generally provides weaker\n1527\tdiscrimination and is often more effective for wordlevel or pattern-level inference than precise character-bycharacter reconstruction.\n1528\t\n1529\tThese findings highlight that keystroke sounds can pose\n1530\ta practical privacy risk even without labeled training data\n1531\tor device compromise. However, the proposed approach is\n1532\tpredicated on several assumptions that constrain its applicability, including reliable keystroke segmentation, a typing speed that is not so rapid that keystrokes significantly\n1533\toverlap, and adequate signal quality under realistic recording conditions. Future work includes improving the robustness of the inference process to environmental noise,\n1534\taddressing keystroke overlaps during high-speed typing,\n1535\tand expanding the diversity of character sets.\n1536\t\n1537\tGeometry-based approaches. A different family of attacks estimates the spatial origin of keystrokes rather than\n1538\tdirectly classifying their content. Liu et al. [23] and Tu\n1539\tet al. [34] used precise time-difference-of-arrival measurements to localize keystroke positions acoustically, while\n1540\tZhu et al. [42] and Yu et al. [39] studied related contextfree or geometric inference settings. Because these approaches recover key locations from geometry, they can\n1541\tbe less dependent on language models and may handle\n1542\tnon-natural-language input more naturally. However, they\n1543\toften require high-precision microphones, multiple sensors, or close-range placement near the target keyboard,\n1544\twhich limits their practicality in opportunistic eavesdropping scenarios.\n1545\t\n1546\tA. Ethical Considerations\n1547\tThis work investigates the feasibility of acoustic eavesdropping attacks that reconstruct typed text from keystroke\n1548\tsounds. While the goal of this research is to improve\n1549\tunderstanding of potential privacy risks, such techniques\n1550\tcould be misused for unauthorized surveillance. We therefore carefully considered ethical implications throughout\n1551\tthe study.\n1552\t\n1553\tVideo-based approaches. Keystroke inference has also\n1554\tbeen studied using visual information. Shukla et al. [31]\n1555\tdemonstrated that hand movements can reveal typed content, Yue et al. [40] studied blind recognition of text input\n1556\ton mobile devices using visual cues and language modeling, and Chen et al. [5] inferred touchscreen input from\n1557\teye movements captured in video. These approaches can\n1558\tbe highly effective when a clear view of the user or device\n1559\tis available, but they require line-of-sight access and are\n1560\tsensitive to occlusion, camera angle, and image quality.\n1561\t\n1562\tResponsible Disclosure. Prior to submission, we contacted relevant stakeholders, including major laptop manufacturers (Apple, Lenovo, Dell, and HP) and online conferencing service providers (Google, Microsoft, and Zoom),\n1563\tto inform them of our findings and provide an opportunity\n1564\tfor response. Our goal is to enable these stakeholders to\n1565\tassess potential risks and consider mitigation strategies.\n1566\t\n1567\tOverall, prior work has established that typed content\n1568\tcan leak through a wide range of side channels. Our work\n1569\tis most closely related to prior unsupervised acoustic approaches, but differs in targeting the limited-observation\n1570\t\n1571\tWe received responses from three vendors. One vendor acknowledged the report and indicated that it is under\n1572\tinternal review. Another vendor stated that the reported\n1573\t13\n1574\t\n1575\t\fText for Experiments under Simplified Condition\n1576\tcomputers have become an essential part of everyday life\n1577\tand their development has a long and interesting history. early computing devices were simple machines designed to assist with calculation. over time these machines\n1578\tevolved into more complex systems that could store and\n1579\tprocess information. the idea of a programmable machine\n1580\tallowed users to perform different tasks using the same\n1581\tdevice, which marked an important step in the growth of\n1582\tcomputing. as computers became more widely used, they\n1583\twere connected through networks that allowed information to be shared across distances. this led to the creation\n1584\tof large systems where people could communicate, store\n1585\tdata, and access services remotely. the growth of networks\n1586\talso introduced new challenges related to security, since\n1587\tinformation could be intercepted or altered during transmission. security in computing developed as a response\n1588\tto these challenges. early methods focused on protecting\n1589\tphysical machines and limiting access to authorized users.\n1590\tas systems became more complex, new techniques were\n1591\tintroduced to protect data and ensure that only intended\n1592\tusers could read or modify information. these techniques\n1593\tinclude methods for verifying identity and controlling access to resources. another important aspect of security is\n1594\tmaintaining the integrity of data. this means ensuring that\n1595\tinformation remains accurate and unchanged unless it is\n1596\tmodified in an authorized way. systems were designed\n1597\tto detect unexpected changes and to recover from errors\n1598\twhen they occur. reliability became a key goal in system\n1599\tdesign, especially as computers were used in critical applications. in modern environments, computers are used in\n1600\tmany different contexts, including communication, education, and entertainment. the widespread use of devices has\n1601\tmade security an ongoing concern. users must be aware\n1602\tof potential risks and systems must be designed to handle\n1603\ta wide range of threats. this includes protecting personal\n1604\tinformation and ensuring that systems continue to operate correctly even under adverse conditions. the history of\n1605\tcomputing and security shows a gradual shift from simple isolated machines to complex interconnected systems.\n1606\teach stage of development introduced new capabilities as\n1607\twell as new challenges. understanding this progression\n1608\thelps explain why security is such an important part of\n1609\tmodern computing systems and why continued research\n1610\tin this area remains necessary.\n1611\t\n1612\tFigure 14: Screenshot of the GUI for keystroke detection.\n1613\t\n1614\tbehavior does not meet their criteria for a security vulnerability requiring servicing. The remaining vendor noted\n1615\tthat the behavior falls within a known class of acoustic\n1616\tside-channel attacks that has been previously studied.\n1617\tTo avoid prematurely attributing impact or creating confusion regarding vendor-specific security status, we do not\n1618\tdisclose vendor identities in this paper. We coordinated the\n1619\ttiming of this publication to align with responsible disclosure practices and to ensure that vendors had an opportunity to review our findings in advance.\n1620\t\n1621\tData Collection and Privacy. All experimental data\n1622\tused in this study were generated by the authors. The\n1623\trecorded keystroke sounds correspond only to text typed\n1624\tby the authors themselves, and do not include any data\n1625\tfrom third-party users. No personally identifiable information (PII) or sensitive user data were collected or used\n1626\tin this work.\n1627\t\n1628\tB. GUI for Keystroke Detection\n1629\tFigure 14 shows a screenshot of the lightweight GUI to\n1630\tsupport interactive inspection and correction of keystroke\n1631\tsegmentation described in Section 3.2. The GUI visualizes\n1632\tthe audio waveform together with the mel-spectrogram\n1633\tand detected segmentation boundaries, allowing users to\n1634\tverify whether each keystroke event is properly identified.\n1635\tUsers can manually add, remove, or adjust peak positions\n1636\tthrough simple interactions, and the corrected peak set is\n1637\timmediately reflected in the re-extracted keystroke segments. This tool is used only for validation and refinement\n1638\tof segmentation quality, and does not require any labeled\n1639\ttext information.\n1640\t\n1641\tText 1\n1642\thello team,\n1643\t\n1644\tC. Typed TEXT\n1645\t\n1646\ti hope this message finds you well. i am writing to share\n1647\ta brief update on the current plan and to confirm our next\n1648\tsteps. yesterday i spoke with alice about the schedule, and\n1649\twe agreed to proceed calmly. the draft will be ready soon,\n1650\tand feedback can be collected without pressure. if you\n1651\thave concerns, please reply at your convenience. thank\n1652\tyou for your steady support and cooperation.\n1653\t\n1654\tFor the text-reconstruction experiments, we prepared three\n1655\tEnglish texts typed by the participant. As also described\n1656\tin the main text, these inputs consist of lowercase alphabetic characters (a--z), spaces, periods, commas, and\n1657\tline breaks. All three texts are business-like email messages and include simple proper nouns such as personal\n1658\tnames.\n1659\t\n1660\tsincerely, john smith\n1661\t14\n1662\t\n1663\t\fTable 1: Character-level BERT hyperparameters.\n1664\tHyperparameter\n1665\tVocabulary size\n1666\tHidden size\n1667\tIntermediate size\n1668\t\n1669\tValue\n1670\t29\n1671\t256\n1672\t1024\n1673\t\n1674\tHyperparameter\n1675\t# of Transformer layers\n1676\t# of Attention heads\n1677\tMax sequence length\n1678\t\n1679\tE. Prompt for LLM Correction\n1680\t\n1681\tValue\n1682\t6\n1683\t4\n1684\t256\n1685\t\n1686\t\"Your goal is to reconstruct the\n1687\toriginal text. Follow these\n1688\tinstructions:\\n\"\n1689\t\"1. Correct character errors to form\n1690\tvalid and contextually appropriate\n1691\tEnglish words.\\n\"\n1692\t\"2. Preserve spaces: The positions of\n1693\tspace characters are highly reliable.\n1694\t\"\n1695\t\"3. Do not add or remove them unless\n1696\tabsolutely necessary to correct a word\n1697\tboundary.\\n\"\n1698\t\"4. The input text consists of\n1699\tlowercase letters a-z, spaces,\n1700\tperiods, and commas.\\n\"\n1701\t\"5. Note that only lowercase letters\n1702\tare used; please add periods and\n1703\tcommas appropriately.\\n\"\n1704\t\"6. Output format: Return only the\n1705\tclean, corrected text without any\n1706\tpreamble.\\n\"\n1707\t\"7. The text may be cut off\n1708\tmid-sentence, but please output it as\n1709\tis.\\n\"\n1710\t\n1711\tTable 2: Character-level BERT training settings.\n1712\tHyperparameter\n1713\tEpochs\n1714\tEffective batch size\n1715\tLearning rate\n1716\tLoss function\n1717\t\n1718\tValue\n1719\t10\n1720\t8192\n1721\t3 × 10−4\n1722\tCross entropy\n1723\t\n1724\tText 2\n1725\tdear michael,\n1726\tthis is a quick follow up regarding our recent discussion.\n1727\tafter reviewing the notes with you, i believe the direction is\n1728\tclear and practical. the task list has been adjusted, and the\n1729\ttimeline should remain comfortable for all members. we\n1730\tcan meet again later to refine details. please let me know\n1731\tif paris time works for you, or if another option is better. i\n1732\tappreciate your patience and trust.\n1733\tsarah johnson\n1734\t\n1735\t\"Now, correct the following text:\\n\"\n1736\tf\"Original text:\n1737\t'{text_to_correct}'\\n\\n\"\n1738\t\"Corrected text:\"\n1739\t\n1740\tText 3\n1741\thello all,\n1742\ti wanted to send a short message to keep communication\n1743\tsmooth. early this morning, i confirmed the status with\n1744\tsarah and david regarding preparations for the new product launching next month, everything appears stable. there\n1745\tis no urgent action required right now, but small improvements are welcome. we aim to maintain quality while\n1746\tmoving forward steadily. feel free to share ideas anytime.\n1747\t\n1748\tReferences\n1749\t[1] Kamran Ali, Alex X Liu, Wei Wang, and Muhammad\n1750\tShahzad. Keystroke recognition using wifi signals.\n1751\tIn Proceedings of the 21st annual international conference on mobile computing and networking, pages\n1752\t90–102, 2015.\n1753\t\n1754\tbest regards, michael taylor\n1755\t\n1756\t[2] Dmitri Asonov and Rakesh Agrawal. Keyboard\n1757\tacoustic emanations. In IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy, 2004. Proceedings. 2004, pages 3–\n1758\t11. IEEE, 2004.\n1759\t\n1760\tD. Training Details for BERT\n1761\tWe train a character-level BERT model using a masked\n1762\tlanguage modeling (MLM) objective. The training corpus is derived from OpenWebText and preprocessed to retain only the target character set (lowercase letters, space,\n1763\tcomma, and period). The dataset is split into 95% for\n1764\ttraining and 5% for validation.\n1765\t\n1766\t[3] Seyyed Ali Ayati and Jin Hyun Park. Making acoustic side-channel attacks on noisy keyboards viable\n1767\twith llm-assisted spectrograms’ “typo” correction. In\n1768\t19th USeNIX WOOT conference on offensive technologies (WOOT 25), pages 87–101, 2025.\n1769\t\n1770\tEach input is tokenized at the character level and converted into a sequence of character IDs. During training,\n1771\t15% of tokens are randomly replaced with [MASK], and\n1772\tthe model is trained to predict the original characters using\n1773\ta cross-entropy loss.\n1774\t\n1775\t[4] Yigael Berger, Avishai Wool, and Arie Yeredor. Dictionary attacks using keyboard acoustic emanations.\n1776\tIn Proceedings of the 13th ACM conference on Computer and communications security, pages 245–254,\n1777\t2006.\n1778\t\n1779\tTable 1 summarizes the model architecture, and Table 2\n1780\tshows the training configuration.\n1781\t\n1782\t[5] Yimin Chen, Tao Li, Rui Zhang, Yanchao Zhang,\n1783\tand Terri Hedgpeth. Eyetell: Video-assisted touchscreen keystroke inference from eye movements. In\n1784\t2018 IEEE Symposium on Security and Privacy (SP),\n1785\tpages 144–160. IEEE, 2018.\n1786\t\n1787\tAll experiments are conducted on a compute environment equipped with dual AMD EPYC 9355 CPUs, eight\n1788\tNVIDIA RTX 6000 Ada GPUs, and 256 GB of memory.\n1789\t15\n1790\t\n1791\t\f[6] The SciPy community. find peaks — scipy v1.17.0\n1792\tmanual.\n1793\thttps://docs.scipy.org/doc/\n1794\tscipy/reference/generated/scipy.\n1795\tsignal.find_peaks.html, 2025. Accessed:\n1796\t2026-03-15.\n1797\t\n1798\t[17] Godfrey N Lance and William Thomas Williams.\n1799\tA general theory of classificatory sorting strategies:\n1800\t1. hierarchical systems. Comput. J., 9(4):373–380,\n1801\t1967.\n1802\t[18] Mengyuan Li, Yan Meng, Junyi Liu, Haojin Zhu,\n1803\tXiaohui Liang, Yao Liu, and Na Ruan. When csi\n1804\tmeets public wifi: Inferring your mobile phone password via wifi signals. In Proceedings of the 2016\n1805\tACM SIGSAC conference on computer and communications security, pages 1068–1079, 2016.\n1806\t\n1807\t[7] Jacob Devlin, Ming-Wei Chang, Kenton Lee, and\n1808\tKristina Toutanova. Bert: Pre-training of deep bidirectional transformers for language understanding.\n1809\tIn Proceedings of the 2019 conference of the North\n1810\tAmerican chapter of the association for computational linguistics: human language technologies,\n1811\tvolume 1 (long and short papers), pages 4171–4186,\n1812\t2019.\n1813\t\n1814\t[19] librosa development team.\n1815\tlibrosa.feature.mfcc\n1816\t— librosa 0.11.0 documentation.\n1817\thttps:\n1818\t//librosa.org/doc/main/generated/\n1819\tlibrosa.feature.mfcc.html, 2025. Accessed: 2026-03-15.\n1820\t\n1821\t[8] Vivek Dhakal, Anna Maria Feit, Per Ola Kristensson, and Antti Oulasvirta. Observations on typing\n1822\tfrom 136 million keystrokes. In Proceedings of the\n1823\t2018 CHI conference on human factors in computing\n1824\tsystems, pages 1–12, 2018.\n1825\t\n1826\t[20] librosa\n1827\tdevelopment\n1828\tteam.\n1829\tlibrosa.feature.spectral centroid — librosa 0.11.0\n1830\tdocumentatio